    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41528&lang=en": {
      "title": "Principal Career Consultant",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/10/2026 Posted Date: Oct 26, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: Business School Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title : Academic Services Principal Professional Working Title: Principal Career Consultant FTE: Full Time 1 (100%) FTE | Hybrid Salary Range: $72,028 - $79,610 Position #00802117– Requisition #41528 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado - Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * This position requires a high level of professionalism, senior leader interactions, excellent written and verbal communication skills, and tact & meeting facilitation. Jobs in this career family provide direct professional support for the teaching and/or educational service mission of the university. Functions include academic advising/counseling, student personnel administration, recruitment and admissions, registration, financial aid, faculty affairs, library services, curriculum administration, and others involving direct support of the educational mission of an academic unit. Principal Career Consultant What you will do: Meet with undergraduate and graduate Business School students and alumni to advise on Resumes, LinkedIn Profiles, Job and Industry Targeting, Networking, Interviewing, and other pertinent career and professional development topics. Ensure students are aware of career opportunities, and employers are receiving quality candidates. Serve as a liaison between students, the university, university services (CU Denver Career Center, Experiential Learning Center, etc.), and Business School Departments (RMI, Jake Jabs Center for Entrepreneurship, Academic Advising, Admissions, J.P. Morgan Center for Commodities, CITI, etc.). Interact with faculty, school clubs, associations, strategic employer partners, and other clients of the Business School to promote connections in the Business School. Through networking and relationship building, strives to connect students and employers for mutually beneficial relationships. Plan, implement, and lead in student and alumni outreach events, which include workshops, career fairs, and job events, information sessions, and other Business School club events. Stay ahead of industry trends, mentor others on the career team, and propel the direction, coaching, and momentum of the team informally Please note, this is not an exhaustive list of responsibilities and roles. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) A bachelor’s degree in a directly related field from an accredited institution. Five (5) years of experience in education, career advising, recruiting, human resources, student support, professional development, or related field Substitution: A combination of education and related technical/paraprofessional experience may be substituted for the bachelor’s degree on a year for year basis. Preferred Qualifications Master’s or Terminal Degree in Counseling, Business, or a directly related field from an accredited institution. Experience providing career coaching, career advising, or professional development support, including guidance on resumes, LinkedIn profiles, interviewing, networking, and job-search strategies. Experience in recruiting, talent acquisition, human resources, or hiring, with an understanding of employer expectations and hiring practices. Knowledge of business career pathways, industry trends, and internship and full-time recruiting practices. Experience working with students, alumni, and professionals of all career levels (early-career, mid-career, director-level, and executive level) Experience facilitating career-readiness workshops, presentations, or other professional development programming. Experience using career services platforms, applicant tracking systems, and job board sites. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Ability to communicate effectively, both in writing and orally. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with employees at all levels throughout the institution. Outstanding customer service skills. Demonstrated commitment and leadership ability to advance diversity and inclusion. Experience working with individuals from diverse backgrounds. Knowledge of and contacts within the business community and skill utilizing Colorado-based businesses and professional organizations for networking pipelines. Ability to establish and maintain external relationships with businesses and other employers and with graduates of the Business School. Excellent MS Office skills (Outlook TM, Word TM, Excel, PowerPoint TMM), with knowledge of LinkedIn and other social networking applications. Ability to work independently with excellent follow-up and self-starter skills. Well-organized and self-confident, with excellent professional presentation skills. Strong attention to detail in all work products. Conditions of Employment This position follows a hybrid work structure three days in office, two days remote weekly. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position will be performed in a hybrid environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in an in person or remote setting. Remote work requires maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $72,028 - $79,610 The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until Nov 9, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications A current CV/resume List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to Tilda Gross at tilda.gross@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41533&lang=en": {
      "title": "Lecturer – J.P Morgan Center for Commodities & Energy Management",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/25/2027 Posted Date: Oct 26, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: Business School Schedule: Part-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Lecturer Working Title: Lecturer – J.P. Morgan Center for Commodities & Energy Management FTE: Part-Time, Less than 50% (0.50) FTE | Remote Salary Range: $8,000+ (Dependent on enrollment) Position # 00805334 – Requisition #41533 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . About the CU Denver Business School “Denver Built. Global Ready”. At the CU Denver Business School, we make the most of being deeply embedded in the heart of Denver and the nation’s fastest growing economy. You’ll find a welcoming, inclusive community that encourages what you bring with you and what you aspire to be. We’re ready to equip any future – locally and globally. We offer more choices than any other Business School in Colorado, with 14 Undergraduate Majors, 4 Industry Programs, 7 MBA Options, 3 Centers of Excellence, and 11 Master of Science Programs. We rank at the top for Social Mobility in Colorado, maintaining over three hundred Business relationships in Colorado and across the country. 80% of our career-minded students work while enrolled, opening doors and lowering barriers to success. CU Denver Business School is the #1 top-ranked national university in Colorado for campus ethnic diversity (US News). Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers* The CU Denver Business School seeks individuals with demonstrated commitment to creating an inclusive learning and working environment. We value the ability to engage effectively with students, faculty and staff of diverse backgrounds. The J.P. Morgan Center for Commodities & Energy Management at the CU Denver Business School seeks applicants for a pool of Lecturers from which future appointments to temporary, non-tenure-track positions will be made. Appointments are part-time (less than 50% FTE) and will occur as teaching needs arise. This posting does not necessarily reflect a current, open position. This temporary Lecturer pool is anticipated to remain continuously active through Jan 2027, at which point all applications will be cleared from the pool. Applicants will be welcome to apply to future pools after that date. Lecturer – J.P Morgan Center for Commodities & Energy Management What you will do: Lecturers selected from this pool will teach classes in their area of expertise with application to Commodities and/or Energy Management related occupations. Duties include delivery of tailored instruction to a variety of language and cultural backgrounds in accordance with prescribed curricular objectives and outcomes, preparing for each class ahead of time with balanced rigorous lesson plans, assessing student progress according to curricular objectives and outcomes, and adhering to program policies and procedures by reporting student offenses (plagiarism, cheating, absences, etc.). Classes taught will be at the graduate level and will generally be limited to three credit hours. Lecturers teaching these classes are expected to instruct remotely but may be asked to instruct in other or multiple teaching modalities (in-person, online-remote, or hybrid). Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Master’s or terminal degree in a business or subject-related field. Recent significant professional, technical, or managerial experience in the discipline related to the teaching assignment; at a minimum, significant experience is interpreted as at least five years of duties and responsibilities. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Prior experience working in the capacity of a consultant, preferably with an ongoing position that is client facing. Prior experience teaching or guest lecturing in a graduate program on the topic of business. Previous experience collaborating with MBA-level projects, either as a facilitator, participant, or client. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge and proficiency in related discipline. Demonstrated interest in mentoring and teaching in related discipline. Demonstrate an exceptional understanding of diversity, equity, and inclusion with a commitment to developing equitable practices. Ability to communicate effectively, both in writing and orally. Ability to demonstrate exceptional leadership qualities. Ability to effectively plan, establish and implement teaching goals and objectives independently without guidance and direction from other faculty. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with employees at all levels throughout the institution. Conditions of Employment Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. This position requires remote work. Lecturers may have opportunities to engage onsite instruction as determined by the modality of evolving and new programming. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed on site and will require the ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. This position may be performed in a hybrid or remote environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $8,000 per 3-credit graduate course and may increase depending on course enrollment at census date. As a part-time teaching role, this position is not eligible for benefits. Information on University benefits programs, including eligibility, is located at Employee Services.Compensation for non-3-credit appointments will be set at a pro-rated amount and based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Application Deadline Applications for this pooled position will be accepted on a rolling basis for up to Three (3) months from the posting date [Oct 26, 2026]. Qualified applicants may be reviewed and approved for hire at any point during this period. Early application is encouraged, as the pool may close before the three‑month window if all openings are filled. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to Brandy Leggins-Baker, brandy.leggins-baker@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=38857&lang=en": {
      "title": "Pipe Mechanical Trades - II Facilities Maintenance Technician Lead (Housing)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/10/2026 Posted Date: Oct 26, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: ADM AVCFM BMO Anschutz Schedule: Full-time University of Colorado Denver Department: Facilities Management Job Title: Pipe Mechanical Trades - II Facilities Maintenance Technician Lead (Housing) Position #: 844879 – Requisition #: 38857 Job Summary: Join the Facilities Management Department on the University of Colorado Denver Campus as a Pipe Mechanical Trades II (PMTII), Facilities Maintenance Technician Lead (Housing). This is a full-time position. The PMT-II Facilities Maintenance Technician Lead (Housing) position: Maintain all facilities and operating systems in a safe and efficient working order. This includes regular maintenance on HVAC, electrical, mechanical, utility systems, interior finishes, and appliances and coordinate work with journey level technicians and contractors. You will record issues and make work reports via email, log your work in CMMS and monitor building systems in BAS and perform various other duties and tasks as required. This is an evening, second shift position. Hours are Monday through Friday, 9:30am to 6:00pm , and qualify for shift differential. Key Responsibilities: 30% - Zone Preventative Maintenance Receives preventive maintenance work orders, performs preventive maintenance on assigned equipment, which may include the following: faucets, pipes, hot water tanks, toilets, garbage disposals, dishwashers, refrigerators, ceiling lights, and logs reports as required The position must have the ability to perform data entry and be able to operate a computer to log reports. The position will record issues through the shift desk computer and maintain the logbook. The position must have the ability for written communication since the position will maintain the logbook. Set priorities based on urgency and make or arrange for the necessary repairs, including replacing or modifying components (e.g., plumbing, steam, electrical, HVAC, and workspace aesthetic-related items). Evaluate operations and implement recommendations to prevent failures and improve efficiency of HVAC and energy management systems. Maintain and monitor operating logs to determine irregular equipment performance 20% - Corrective Maintenance Respond to corrective maintenance calls and verbal assignments, ensuring that tenant/staff requests are attended to in a timely, courteous, and efficient manner. These work orders include requests for the repair of minor electrical items, including lighting, minor plumbing items, floods, freezer monitoring, HVAC items, including hot/cold calls, and too much/too little air supply. Respond to, and communicate with, tenants to ensure all complaints are resolved in a timely manner and in a craftsman-like fashion. The position will operate a university vehicle to transport necessary tools/equipment to work sites across campus 10% - Project Coordination and Support Assist in Capital Projects as appropriate to ensure quality construction and repairs. Coordinate Facilities Funded Projects within the Zone. Collaborate and advise project managers, general contractors, and vendors during construction, occupancy and warranty phases to ensure all mechanical and control problems are installed/repaired to meet UCD|AMC construction standards. Provide BO&M representation as required. Act as a Subject Matter Expert where appropriate and provide feedback to Project Manager via responses. 10% - Emergencies, Special Events and Other Duties Participates and assists in emergencies based on the magnitude and nature. Supports various special events both in preparation and during the event (i.e. Commencement). Examples of emergencies and special events could include commencement, snow removal, power outages, flood, etc. May be assigned other duties as required. The position must have the ability to communicate with security, fire department, and the police department in case of an emergency and be able to communicate the problem. 30% - Lead Responsibilities Coordinates the team’s tasks, manages procurement, reviews WO entries, and assists team members as needed. Implements leadership priorities to support vision and objectives. Work Location: Onsite – this role is expected to work onsite and is located in Denver, Colorado. Why Join Us: Apply today to be part of a team where the focus is supporting the university’s mission of education the next generation. We are looking for someone with a solid maintenance/service background to learn more and develop new skills. You will be set up for success. We will provide you with the training necessary to become proficient in the equipment we maintain. There are also paid external training opportunities for beneficial technical and professional development courses. All of the hand and specialty tools you need will be provided. We offer robust training program for anyone interested in growing their career options. From hands on work with journey level technicians and online self-paced technical training to paid external trades certifications and CU employee tuition assistance, CU Denver can be the place to boost your career trajectory. Come to a place where you can develop a career, not just another job. For more information about the university of Colorado Denver campus please visit www.ucdenver.edu Why work for the University? We have AMAZING benefits and offer exceptional amounts of holiday, vacation and sick leave! The University of Colorado offers an excellent benefits package including: Medical: Multiple plan options Dental: Multiple plan options Additional Insurance: Disability, Life, Vision Retirement 401(a) Plan: Employer contributes 10% of your gross pay Paid Time Off: Accruals over the year Vacation Days: 22/year (maximum accrual 352 hours) Sick Days: 15/year (unlimited maximum accrual) Holiday Days: 10/year Tuition Benefit: Employees have access to this benefit on all CU campuses ECO Pass: Reduced rate RTD Bus and light rail service There are many additional perks & programs with the CU Advantage . Qualifications: Minimum Qualifications: Three years of progressively responsible residential maintenance experience, including minor repairs to HVAC, electrical, lighting, and plumbing systems and indoor finishes. Colorado law requires that candidates for state government jobs be residents of Colorado at the time of application. Substitution: Successful completion of a formalized plumbing, pipefitting, steam fitting, or heating, ventilation, and air conditioning apprenticeship program or higher level, non-correspondence course of work in the same field from an accredited vocational school or university may substitute for the experience on a year-to-year basis. Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Preferred Qualifications: 1 -3 years of lead experience Experience using computers to monitor buildings tied into Building Automation Systems (BAS) Experience fixing and maintaining equipment and machinery Experience keeping activity logs Experience in heating, ventilation, and air conditioning Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Ability to manage multiple tasks and work independently with minimal supervision. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to establish rapport with people of diverse backgrounds. Experience that demonstrates strong customer service skills. Excellent oral communication skills with the ability to handle stressful situations in a calm and professional manner. Ability to communicate effectively in written form, which includes the ability to check for typographical, grammatical, and spelling errors Mechanical Ability Physical Ability Planning and organization skills. Knowledge of heating, ventilation, and air conditioning How to Apply: For full consideration, please submit the following document(s): 1. A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position (only use if a cover letter is necessary) 2. Curriculum vitae / Resume 3. Three to five professional references, including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address Questions should be directed to: Megan Freije megan.freije@cuanschutz.edu Screening of Applications Begins: Immediately and continues until position is filled. For best consideration, apply by November 9, 2026. Anticipated Pay Range: The starting salary range $4906 - $5690 per month for this position has been established. The second shift differential of 7.5% shall be paid for all hours worked when half or more of the scheduled work hours fall between 4 p.m. and 11 p.m. Monday through Thursday. The above salary range ( or hiring range ) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position may be eligible for overtime compensation, depending on the level. This position may be eligible for a one-time non-base building sign-on bonus up to $2,000. Details of this recruitment incentive program will be provided during the interview process. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Total Compensation Calculator Equal Employment Opportunity Statement: CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. ADA Statement: The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at hr.adacoordinator@cuanschutz.edu ​. Background Check Statement: The University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus is dedicated to ensuring a safe and secure environment for our faculty, staff, students and visitors. To assist in achieving that goal, we conduct background investigations for all prospective employees. Vaccination Statement: CU Anschutz strongly encourages vaccination against the COVID-19 virus and other vaccine preventable diseases . If you work, visit, or volunteer in healthcare facilities or clinics operated by our affiliated hospital or clinical partners or by CU Anschutz, you will be required to comply with the vaccination and medical surveillance policies of the facilities or clinics where you work, visit, or volunteer, respectively. In addition, if you work in certain research areas or perform certain safety sensitive job duties, you must enroll in the occupational health medical surveillance program ."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41485&lang=en": {
      "title": "Temporary Career Counselor",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/06/2026 Posted Date: Oct 22, 2026 Job category: Student/Temp Department: AVCSCE-Career Services Schedule: Part-time University of Colorado Denver Department: Denver Career Services Job Title: Temporary Career Counselor This is a Temporary position limited to no more than 9-months. Job Summary: This position provides comprehensive career counseling and advising to a diverse body of undergraduate students, graduate students, and alumni regarding goal setting, major/career direction, skill analysis, résumé preparation, job search, networking strategies, and interviewing skills. The part-time career counselor will provide one-on-one advising by meeting with undergraduate and graduate students and alumni through drop-in and scheduled appointments. The part-time career counselor will plan and complete Boots to Suits sessions through May 2027 and complete class/group presentations. Key responsibilities: · Lead career-focused presentations for classes other campus groups · Provide career counseling for students; assess students’ individual needs and direct them to appropriate services as needed · Attend Career Services team meetings remotely · May include supporting on-campus programs such as Boots to Suits Work Location: Hybrid – this role is eligible for a hybrid schedule of # days per week on campus and as needed for in-person meetings. Why Join Us: The CU Denver Career Center is a collaborative, student-focused office that supports students and alumni in exploring career options, gaining professional experience, and achieving their career goals. We serve a diverse student population across all majors and career stages through individualized career counseling, career education, employer partnerships, and experiential learning opportunities. We seek a part-time Career Counselor who is committed to a student-centered, empathetic approach, providing personalized guidance that empowers students to build confidence and make informed decisions as they navigate their career journeys. Qualifications: Minimum Qualifications: Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. · A Bachelor’s degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Psychology, Communication, Sociology, Counseling, Liberal Arts, Education, Human Service, Student Affairs, or a directly related field from an accredited institution. · Two (2) years of professional level experience in career counseling, coaching, and/or advising in a higher education setting (internships may be included). Preferred Qualifications: · A Master’s degree in Counseling, Student Affairs, Higher Education or a directly related field · Two (2) years of experience career counseling, coaching, advising, and/or generally supporting culturally and ethnically diverse individual’s development in Higher Education or college setting (excluding internship). · Experience attending and/or facilitating workshops, professional training and/or certificate/licensure in the MBTI, SII, and/or GallupStrengths. · Experience meeting with online students and facilitating virtual programming such as presentations and workshops. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities · Strong counseling and natural leadership skills, ability to connect with students and assess their needs. · Exceptional presentation skills and ability to facilitate discussions. · Experienced with Handshake or other career management platform · Outstanding customer service skills. · Proficient with technology such as Microsoft Office 365 and videoconference/webcam (Zoom, Google Hangouts, etc.). · Ability to communicate effectively, both in writing and orally. · Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with employees at all levels throughout the institution. · Demonstrated commitment and leadership ability to advance diversity and inclusion How to Apply: For full consideration, please submit the following document(s): 1. A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position 2. Curriculum vitae / Resume 3. Five professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers . Questions should be directed to: Joan Fishburn, Joan.Fishburn@ucdenver.edu Screening of Applications Begins: Immediately and continues until November 5, 2026. Anticipated Pay Range: The hiring rate for this position has been established at $28.00 / hour. The above salary rate represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the rate at the time of posting. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. Temporary employees are only eligible for sick leave benefits. Equal Employment Opportunity Statement: The University of Colorado (CU) is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. ADA Statement: The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at hr.adacoordinator@ucdenver.edu ​. Background Check Statement: The University of Colorado Denver is dedicated to ensuring a safe and secure environment for our faculty, staff, students and visitors. To assist in achieving that goal, we conduct background investigations for all prospective employees."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41477&lang=en": {
      "title": "Graduate Contracts and Finance Coordinator",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/05/2026 Posted Date: Oct 21, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: VCASA/EVCASA Academic&Stdnt Af Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Academic Services Senior Professional Working Title: Graduate Contracts and Finance Coordinator FTE: Full-time 1.0 (100%) FTE | Hybrid | Term – Limited Salary Range : $63,000 - $65,846 Position #00853742 – Requisition #41477 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . *This is a term limited position that is currently funded for 1.5 Years (18 Months).* Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The position will be reviewed after 18 months of employment to assess financial sustainability. If your term of employment will be concluded based on a lack of funding, you will be provided 30 days’ notice of the term of employment in writing by the University. About the GSO The newly reconstituted Graduate Studies Office (GSO) supports graduate students, faculty, and programs at CU Denver by defining and maintaining campus wide graduate policies and procedures; coordinating administration of graduate assistantships and tuition remission; overseeing academic progress and degree completion; and advancing enrollment, retention, and student engagement initiatives. The Graduate Student Contracts and Finance Coordinator is a student- and staff-focused position that provides leadership and support for all financial administration activities of the Graduate Studies Office at the University of Colorado Denver. Job functions include tasks like coordinating with finance and HR offices across campus to develop and maintain campus-consistent, transparent processes for awarding and administering student assistantships and tuition remission; administering dissemination of student fellowship and travel awards; coordinating across the Denver and AMC campuses to assist in developing structural support for student and faculty applications for research and training grants; for student and faculty training in responsible research conduct; for graduate student professional development; and other activities that directly support the research and training missions of the Graduate Studies Office. Supervision of student employees may be required. Graduate Contracts and Finance Coordinator What you will do: Graduate Student Assistantships and Tuition Remission Partner with HR, registrar, and the office of institutional research and effectiveness to develop procedures for centralized review and approval of graduate student assistantships and for determining tuition remission eligibility. Partner with school and college staff to support transition and stabilization for graduate student hiring policies and practices. Partner with local and central finance and the bursar to provide tuition remission for eligible student assistantships. Student Services and Finance Support Manage emails related to graduate student appointments, fellowships, and travel award requests submitted to the graduateeducation@ucdenver.edu inbox. Identify opportunities to improve workflows, documentation, and service delivery. Research and Professional Development Partner with office of research development, office of strategic enrollment and student success, and AMC staff to identify and act on opportunities to improve graduate student and graduate faculty training in research and professional development and in responsible research conduct Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Bachelor’s degree from an accredited college or university, or 4 years of relevant experience in an occupation related to the work assigned to this position. Two (2) years of experience in higher education administrative support. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Four (4) years of experience in higher education administrative support. Master’s or terminal degree from an accredited college or university. Experience with university personnel, financial aid, and finance management software platforms. Familiarity with diversity of graduate research and creative activity programs. Experience coordinating cross-disciplinary teams in higher education settings. Prior experience working in the Graduate Studies Office or Graduate School at CU Denver. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Ability to communicate effectively. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with employees at all levels across the university. Ability to perform duties independently without guidance and direction from other professionals. Ability to manage multiple projects and tasks that require high attention to detail. Ability to manage day-to-day workflow, administration, and oversight of project processes. Conditions of Employment This is a term-limited position that is currently funded for 18 months Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed on site, and will require the ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. This position may be performed in a hybrid or remote environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $63,000 - $65,846. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Nov 4, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications A current CV/resume List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to: Tyler Keyworth, tyler.keyworth@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41419&lang=en": {
      "title": "Lecturer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 01/13/2027 Posted Date: Oct 14, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CLAS-Ethnic Studies Schedule: Part-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Lecturer Working Title: Lecturer FTE : part-time Salary Range: The hiring rate for this position has been established at $1,704 per credit hour for undergraduate courses per semester or $1,849 per credit hour per graduate courses per semester. Position #00653636– Requisition #41419 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * Lecturer What you will do: This posting will establish and maintain a pool of part-time lecturers with experience teaching general Ethnic Studies courses, such as the Introduction to Ethnic Studies, Indigenous Studies and courses within their area of expertise, for the Department of Ethnic Studies in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Colorado Denver. Lecturers may be asked to teach on campus or fully online. By submitting your application, you will become part of an applicant pool. Candidates hired from this pool will fill temporary, non-tenure track positions and will teach no more than two courses per semester. Appointments will be part-time (less than 50%) and will be made semester-by-semester, depending on the needs of the department and subject to budgetary approval. Therefore, this posting does not necessarily reflect a current open position. We will contact you if an opening suits your qualifications. The Downtown campus is located in the heart of the city's commercial, cultural and recreational district. Its location in downtown Denver attracts a diverse population, comprising both traditional and non-traditional students. This diversity is enriched by our commitment to a philosophy of inclusion, embedded in all aspects of campus life from its community members, curriculum, research and community service, allowing for the creation of a learning environment that welcomes, embraces, sustains and celebrates the unique and shared experiences of faculty, staff, and students. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Master's degree in Ethnic Studies or related field Substitution: A combination of education and related technical/paraprofessional experience may be substituted for the bachelor’s degree on a year for year basis. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Experience teaching Ethnic Studies courses. Experience teaching online using the Canvas LMS. Conditions of Employment This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Compensation and Benefits The hiring rate for this position has been established at $1,704 per credit hour for undergraduate courses per semester or $1,849 per credit hour per graduate courses per semester. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications for this pooled position will be accepted on a rolling basis for up to three (3) months from the posting date [October 14, 2026]. Qualified applicants may be reviewed and approved for hire at any point during this period. Early application is encouraged, as the pool may close before the three‑month window if all openings are filled. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). Five professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). Questions should be directed to Edelina Burciaga, Edelina.Burciaga@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41345&lang=en": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor – Criminal Justice",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/31/2026 Posted Date: Oct 14, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: SPA - OTHER Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Assistant Professor Working Title: Assistant Professor – Criminal Justice FTE: Full-Time Salary Range: $76,000 - $87,000 Position #00008187– Requisition #41345 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit cudenver.edu. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The University of Colorado Denver seeks individuals with demonstrated commitment to creating an inclusive learning and working environment. We value the ability to engage effectively with students, faculty and staff of diverse backgrounds. The nationally-ranked, NASPAA accredited School of Public Affairs (SPA) at the University of Colorado Denver seeks a full-time 9-month/academic year Assistant Professor for the CJ programs within SPA, starting fall of 2027. The standard teaching load is four courses over the academic year in person and online (2 in each semester). There may be additional summer teaching opportunities available as a separate contract as well. The candidate must be able to teach in our BACJ and MCJ cores, as well as some elective courses within the CJ programs. A strong research trajectory is required. The successful candidate will be expected to develop and sustain an active program of high-quality research, including actively pursuing external funding. Experience with externally funded research, including participation in or leadership of grant proposals, is highly desirable. We are seeking a very collegial scholar, who feels comfortable doing CJ research/teaching within a broader applied social science/public affairs school setting. Experience working with scholars from other related fields is a plus. Assistant Professor – Criminal Justice What you will do: Teaching: Teach courses online and in-person as needed by SPA Teach courses at both the Undergraduate and Graduate (master’s) level as needed by SPA May teach cross-listed courses with Public Administration, and Public Policy programs as needed by SPA Areas of expertise and teaching areas that we are looking for could include, but are not limited to: Criminological Theory, Interpersonal or Community Violence, Courts, Victimology, Statistics and Research Methods, Data Visualization, GIS, Mixed Method Design, and Practice-Informed Analysis. Service & Leadership Contribute to the ongoing growth of the Criminal Justice program through committee service, student mentorship (formal academic advising provided by professional advisors in the School), serving as a second reader on capstone projects, and positively contributing to SPA programs Research To help us achieve our campus goal to become internationally known for our research and creative work, the successful candidate will be expected to develop and sustain a strong program of high-quality research and contribute to the overall research agenda of the School of Public Affairs. Expectations include producing peer-reviewed scholarship and actively pursuing external funding. Interest and experience in collaborative and interdisciplinary research are desirable. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. PhD in Criminal Justice, Criminology, Public Administration, Public Policy, Political Science, Public Affairs, Sociology, or a closely related field. Applicants must have teaching experience and effectiveness in Criminal Justice, Criminology, Public Affairs/Public Policy, and/or Sociology programs. A strong research trajectory. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Demonstrated ability/experience teaching online classes Success and/or experience with grant funded research Experience designing and teaching courses with a university learning management system (preferably Canvas) Ability and experience teaching at the graduate and undergraduate level. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Willingness to contribute to the holistic growth of the Criminal Justice Program Ability to communicate effectively, both in writing and orally. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with employees at all levels throughout the institution. Outstanding student service skills and a student-centered attitude Demonstrated commitment and leadership ability to advance diversity and inclusion. Conditions of Employment This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. The search committee may conduct a limited number of preliminary interviews during the ASC Annual Meeting. These interviews may be scheduled through and conducted in the ASC Employment Exchange designated interview area. Participation in conference-based interviews is optional and will not adversely affect consideration for this position. Applicants who are not attending the conference will have alternative interview opportunities available. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed in a hybrid or remote environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is eligible now and in the future for visa sponsorship. While the hiring unit is willing to pursue visa sponsorship, an offer of employment will be contingent on the candidate having a visa status that allows employment with the University as of the start date for the position or the University’s petition being approved and on the candidate being able to obtain the appropriate visa status. Compensation and Benefits The starting salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established as HIRING RANGE: $76,000 - $87,000 per academic year depending on experience and other qualifications. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Monday, November 9, 2026 a 11:59:00 PM. . Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: For full consideration, please submit the following document(s): A letter of interest describing relevant teaching experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and specifying any experience or abilities that you meet in our preferred qualifications list. The letter should speak to your ability and/or experience teaching students across different modalities (in-person, online, hybrid, etc). Curriculum vitae / Resume List of three to five professional references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Any FCQs or student evaluations for the last 2 years, if available. Questions should be directed to: Chris Smith at chris.smith@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41374&lang=en": {
      "title": "Creative Technology Specialist",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/14/2026 Posted Date: Oct 14, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: LIB LIBRARY Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Academic Services Intermediate Professional Working Title: Creative Technology Specialist FTE: Full time 1.0 (100%) FTE | Hybrid Salary Range: $58031 - $64140 Position #00716247 – Requisition #41374 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado - Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top ranked bachelors, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $800 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Creative Technology Specialist will support the work of the Innovation Garage and Digital Media Studio, two of the Auraria Library’s dynamic service points. This position works directly with students, faculty, and staff of the Auraria Campus across a variety of areas including graphic design, large format printing, 3D printing, and related makerspace tools, photo and video cameras, and audio equipment. This position provides consulting services in professional resources and tools related to these areas. As one of the primary service providers for the Innovation Garage and Digital Media Studio, the Creative Technology Specialist oversees daily operations, ensuring the consistent application of policies and procedures, supporting the work of Access and User Services colleagues, staff, and student employees. Creative Technology Specialist What you will do: I nnovation Garage Duties Provides expert consulting services in large format printing, vinyl cutting, 3D printing, image digitization, maker tools and other creative technologies. Monitoring email inquiries, booking calendars, assisting users, and maintaining equipment. Maintains a safe and comfortable environment for creative technology service users, thus creating a positive user experience. Provides users with tools and services to bring their ideas to life. Works across the Library to promote understanding and use of Creative Technology Services. Provides formal and informal technology training to library staff and campus faculty, staff, and students. Performs inventory, monitoring, and ordering supplies as needed. Stays current with creative and maker trends through continuing education, research, and environmental scans. Evaluates creative hardware and software and makes upgrade recommendations as part of continual service improvement. Documents and refines workflows and other standard operating procedures. Employs problem-solving and critical thinking skills to arrive at creative solutions to complex issues. Ensures Innovation Garage website content is up to date. Collects, analyzes, and applies data to inform decision-making. Provide recommendations for equipment purchases and repairs. Contributes to a positive working environment. Digital Media Studio Duties Maintains extensive knowledge of and ability to operate DMS photography, video, and audio equipment and related software. Monitoring email inquiries, booking calendar, assisting users, and maintaining equipment. Fulfills appointments by setting up equipment properly and on time, assisting users, maintaining equipment, and creating a positive user experience. Ensures Digital Media Studio website content is up to date. Assists in training other staff on DMS operations and equipment use as needed. Provides informed recommendations for equipment purchases and repairs. Incorporates critical research into DMS practices and services. Additional duties Member of Library committees and task forces as needed. Perform other duties as assigned. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Bachelor's degree in related discipline, such as digital design or film production. 1+ years of professional experience in digital media, educational/creative technology, or related fields. Customer service experience. Substitution: A combination of education and related technical or professional experience may be substituted for the bachelor’s degree on a year-for-year basis. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Experience supervising a service point supporting student success in a higher education setting. Experience creating and maintaining workflow documentation. Experience in a library or higher education environment. Supervisory experience. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Advanced proficiency with Adobe Creative Cloud or related products. Advanced knowledge of and experience working with image and video projects. Advanced knowledge of a variety of makerspace services and equipment. Inventory monitoring experience. Ability to solve technology problems quickly and creatively. Effective learning and presentation skills. Ability to work independently and in team settings. Ability to communicate effectively, both orally and in writing. Experience with Microsoft Office products including Excel, Word, and Outlook. Conditions of Employment Work Hours: Monday-Friday; 8:00am – 5:00pm. Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. This position is primarily in person to support users, equipment, and daily operations, with limited hybrid work available based on the needs of the library. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed in a hybrid or remote environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $58,031 - $64,140. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Nov 13, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications. A current CV/resume. List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). Questions should be directed to Peyton Hertz, at PEYTON.HERTZ@UCDENVER.EDU . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41323&lang=en": {
      "title": "Associate Vice Chancellor for Student Affairs",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/07/2026 Posted Date: Oct 8, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: VCSSLS-AVCSCE UnivAdv&StdEngag Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Associate Vice Chancellor Working Title: Associate Vice Chancellor for Student Affairs FTE: Full-time Salary Range: $200,000-$220,000 Position #00350581 – Requisition #41323 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Associate Vice Chancellor (AVC) for Student Affairs provides strategic and visionary leadership for a comprehensive, student-centered approach to engagement, belonging, wellbeing, and success. The AVC establishes the vision, priorities, strategies, and organizational infrastructure necessary to create an exceptional student experience and ensure students have the support and resources they need to thrive. As a key leader within the Division of Strategic Enrollment and Student Success (SESS), the AVC reports to the Vice Chancellor for Strategic Enrollment and Student Success and serves as a strategic partner to senior leaders across the university. The AVC leads student services and resources that promote personal development, student engagement, wellbeing, and a safe, welcoming, and inclusive campus community. Through these efforts, Student Affairs plays a critical role in advancing student persistence, retention, completion, and overall success. The AVC will bring a deep understanding of the opportunities and challenges facing today’s higher education institutions, particularly those affecting an increasingly diverse student population. The AVC will use data, research, assessment, and student feedback to identify emerging needs, inform decisions, evaluate effectiveness, and address structural barriers that may impede student success. The AVC will also build strong relationships across academic and administrative units and with external partners to strengthen the university’s capacity to serve students effectively. The AVC will be a forward-thinking and entrepreneurial leader who anticipates the evolving needs of students and develops innovative approaches to the student experience. The AVC will strategically prioritize people, programs, technology, and financial resources to maximize student success and institutional impact. The position provides leadership for professional and student staff, has fiscal responsibility for the Student Affairs portfolio and its operating budgets, and leads organizational strategies that promote a high-performing, collaborative, and student-centered culture. As a member of the SESS leadership team, the AVC serves as a campus-wide leader and strategic partner at the intersection of academics, enrollment, student success, wellbeing, and external engagement. The AVC will work across organizational boundaries to align resources, strengthen partnerships, remove barriers, and advance institutional priorities that enable every student to succeed. Associate Vice Chancellor for Student Affairs What you will do: Develop and lead a comprehensive Student Affairs strategy aligned with the university’s mission, strategic plan, enrollment goals, and student success priorities. Translate institutional priorities into measurable goals, strategies, initiatives, and outcomes. Serve as a strategic advisor to senior leadership, including the Senior Vice Chancellor for Strategic Enrollment and Student Success (SESS) and the SESS leadership team, on matters related to the student experience, engagement, campus climate, wellbeing, safety, and retention. Collaborate effectively with deans, Academic Affairs, and campus and external partners. Provide visionary leadership for the student experience, creating a welcoming, inclusive, engaging, and student-centered campus environment. Strengthen programs and services that advance belonging, community, leadership development, engagement, personal growth, wellbeing, and student success. Lead strategic planning, organizational development, and continuous improvement across the Student Affairs portfolio. Streamline functions, processes, practices, and technology to improve service delivery, operational effectiveness, student outcomes, and retention. Use data, research, assessment, and student feedback to identify emerging needs, evaluate programs and services, inform decision-making, and ensure student experiences meaningfully shape institutional policies and priorities. Promote a culture that recognizes students as partners in shaping the university experience. Lead a coordinated student success continuum that advances engagement, retention, wellbeing, and student success through collaboration across CU Denver, the tri-institutional community, AHEC, and external partners. Provide strategic leadership for student wellbeing, mental health, wellness, and conduct, working collaboratively across disciplines to strengthen a proactive campus wellness culture that supports student success. Lead and develop the Student Affairs leadership team, providing supervision, coaching, performance management, professional development, and support for six direct reports and the broader Student Affairs workforce. Foster a collaborative, supportive, high-performing workplace. Provide executive oversight of fiscal and administrative operations, including budget planning, resource allocation, business management, housing assets, contract services, negotiations, and procurement in accordance with university policies and procedures. Leverage internal and external resources and partnerships to strengthen the campus community, enhance the student experience, advance institutional priorities, and elevate the university’s reputation. Travel as needed to support university, community, and professional responsibilities. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Earned Master’s degree in higher education, educational leadership, business administration, public administration or a closely related field Eight (8) years of diverse and progressively responsible experience at the management level, including direct responsibility for student services and/or related services in higher education. Progressive responsibility and experience in supervising professional and administrative staff. Demonstrable experience in managing and working with complex budgets. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Earned doctorate Student Affairs and student success experience at a higher education institution that is a comprehensive urban campus. Documented experience in developing, implementing and evaluating unit budgets, goals and strategic planning. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Strong understanding of university student services and development programs, policies, and practices. Ability to work effectively with diverse teams and individuals and units on and off campus.\\ Demonstrated leadership, budget management and interpersonal skills. A leadership style that promotes collaboration and cooperation. Commitment to excellence, creativity and innovation. Strong oral and written communication skills. Demonstrated ability to communicate and maintain interpersonal relationships within an ethnically and culturally diverse urban learning environment with a commitment to inclusion, diversity and respect. Documented experience in developing, implementing and evaluating unit goals and strategic planning. Computer proficiency including MS Office, social media and CRM experience. Ability to prioritize and perform multiple projects simultaneously. Eagerness to acquire new skills. Dependable, team-oriented and a self-starter. Ability to manage and coach team members and develop staff. Skilled record of accomplishment leading, supervising, evaluating, hiring and resolving staff performance problems. Ability to think strategically and aptitude for managing multiple tasks and priorities. Experience collaborating with multiple stakeholders. Conditions of Employment Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed in a hybrid environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $200,000 - $220,000. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Nov 6, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications A current CV/resume List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to Tyler Keyworth, tyler.keyworth@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41335&lang=en": {
      "title": "(Open Rank) Professor, Director of Construction",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/31/2026 Posted Date: Oct 6, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CEDC-Civil Engineering Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: (Open Rank) Professor, Faculty and Director Working Title: (Open Rank) Professor, Director of Construction FTE: Full-time Salary Range: $85,000.00 - $130,000.00, 9-month contract Position #: 00350978 - Req #41335 About the University of Colorado - Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top ranked bachelors, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $800 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu. About the College The College of Engineering, Design and Computing (CEDC) takes pride in having a faculty of excellent scholars and engineering practitioners. We attract hard-working students who are enthusiastic about engineering, and we are redesigning engineering education to create the agile versatile engineers of the future. Providing an education of enduring value to our students is at the heart of our College mission. We integrate design innovation and computing technology across disciplines, in conjunction with authentic experiences that develop human and social skills such as creativity, collaboration, entrepreneurship, and leadership. We embrace and leverage our setting across urban and medical campuses to broadly impact the social and economic growth of the Denver urban corridor through enterprise partnerships. Our goal is to emerge as Denver’s technological innovation engine and significantly impact Colorado, the nation, and the world via informatics, infrastructure, sustainability, as a smart city. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Department of Civil Engineering and Construction in the College of Engineering, Design and Computing at the University of Colorado Denver invites applications for the position of Director of Construction. We seek an accomplished leader in construction education, research, and/or professional practice to lead CU Denver’s growing Construction Management and Construction Engineering Management programs. The successful candidate will provide strategic leadership for the programs, support and mentor faculty and students, strengthen industry partnerships, and guide continued growth in education, research, and external engagement. We welcome candidates whose careers bridge academia and professional practice, including candidates with substantial leadership experience in the Architecture, Engineering, and Construction (AEC) industry. Candidates do not need to currently hold a tenured faculty appointment to be considered. The successful candidate will receive a faculty appointment in the Department of Civil Engineering and Construction and a separate four-year appointment as Director of Construction. Depending on the candidate’s background and qualifications, the faculty appointment may be as a tenured Associate Professor or Professor or on the Teaching Professor track. Teaching duties will be in construction engineering management, with upcoming needs in Construction Management Fundamentals, Field Engineering and Management, Materials and Methods, Advanced Construction Technology, Construction Safety, and Construction Engineering. Faculty track and rank will be commensurate with the candidate’s education, experience, qualifications, and career path. Why CU Denver and Denver CU Denver's downtown campus places faculty in the heart of a major metropolitan region, with close connections to construction firms, public agencies, professional organizations, and industry partners across Colorado. Denver offers the cultural and professional resources of a major city, with the Rocky Mountains and extensive outdoor recreation nearby. CU Denver combines the resources of the University of Colorado system with an urban campus closely connected to the community it serves. Eligible employees have access to health and retirement benefits, tuition assistance, professional development resources, and RTD EcoPass transit benefits. About the Department The Civil Engineering and Construction department has 10 full-time tenured and tenure-track faculty, 3 teaching-track faculty, and many part-time lecturers. The department offers undergraduate and graduate degrees, including master's and doctoral degrees, in civil engineering, construction engineering management, and construction management. The civil engineering program has been accredited continuously since its inception, and the newer construction programs were first accredited in 2023. In 2025, the department had 75 graduate students and 310 undergraduate students. Approximately 35% are in the construction programs. About the Program The Construction Engineering and Management (CEM) program at the University of Colorado Denver (CU Denver) launched in Fall 2014 as a graduate specialty of the Master of Engineering in Civil Engineering in response to local industry requests and support. Work on two bachelor's degrees began in 2017: the Bachelor of Science (BS) in Construction Management (CM) and the Bachelor of Science in Construction Engineering Management (CEM). CEDC moved forward with both degrees because of interdisciplinary support throughout the College and across CU Denver, especially from the College of Architecture and Planning and the Business School. The two BS degrees were approved by CU Denver, the University of Colorado Regents, and the Colorado Department of Higher Education in Fall 2019, launched in August 2020, and accredited by ABET in 2023. (Open Rank) Professor, Director of Construction What you will do: The faculty responsibilities associated with this position depend on the appointment track and reflect the workload distribution while serving as Director of Construction. A Teaching Professor-track appointment will include 40% teaching and 60% service, while a tenure-line appointment will include 20% teaching, 20% research, and 60% service. The 60% service allocation includes the leadership and administrative responsibilities associated with the four-year Director appointment. The Director appointment includes an additional academic year administrative stipend equal to 7–10% of the faculty base salary. If the Director appointment ends, the faculty workload would be adjusted consistent with the individual’s faculty track and other assigned responsibilities. Lead the Construction Programs Recruit, develop, and retain outstanding faculty and staff Oversee program ABET accreditation; manage operations and budget in partnership with department leadership; lead issue resolution Grow Enrollment and Program Reach Lead strategy to grow and retain undergraduate and graduate enrollment Increase the program’s visibility and reputation regionally and nationally Advance Academic Quality and Student Experience Champion impactful curricular and extracurricular learning opportunities Integrate emerging construction technologies into teaching and learning Strengthen Industry Engagement and Fundraising Continue to build and activate a strong Industry Advisory Board (IAB) to support program relevance, student opportunities, and resource development Diversify program revenue streams Strengthen Research and External Funding Support growth in research activity and external funding across the program Support and mentor faculty in developing research and scholarly activities Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. All candidates: Demonstrated leadership experience in academia, the construction industry, or a closely related setting. For appointment on the tenure line: PhD in Construction Management, Construction Engineering, Civil Engineering, or a related field. A proven record of teaching, research, and service accomplishments commensurate with unit-level and college requirements for appointment with tenure at the Associate Professor or Professor level in the Department of Civil Engineering and Construction. For appointment on the Teaching Professor track: Terminal degree in Construction Management, Construction Engineering, Civil Engineering, or a related field, or equivalent qualifications and professional experience consistent with the applicable Teaching Professor-track appointment. A proven record of teaching and/or professional accomplishments commensurate with unit-level and college requirements for appointment at the applicable rank on the Teaching Professor track in the Department of Civil Engineering and Construction. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Competitive candidates may demonstrate strengths in several of the following areas. Candidates are not expected to possess every preferred qualification. Professional experience in or substantial engagement with the Architecture, Engineering, and Construction (AEC) industry. Experience developing industry partnerships, working with advisory boards, and/or supporting fundraising or external engagement. Experience teaching, training, advising, or mentoring students or professionals in construction-related fields. Experience with accreditation, program assessment, or continuous improvement; ABET experience is preferred. Interest in or experience with emerging construction technologies, including applications of AI in education, research, or professional practice. Professional Engineer licensure or other relevant professional license or certification. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Effective communication, interpersonal, and relationship-building skills. Ability to lead collaboratively and support the professional growth of faculty and staff. Strong commitment to undergraduate and graduate education and student success across a range of backgrounds, educational pathways, and career goals. Ability to guide curriculum and program development in response to changes in construction education and professional practice. Conditions of Employment Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. Working from the office is encouraged particularly when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements The ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position may be eligible now and in the future for certain visa sponsorship limited to Change of Status (COS) within the United States or if the candidate meets the criteria for the portability provision. While the hiring unit is willing to pursue visa sponsorship, an offer of employment will be contingent on the candidate having a visa status that allows employment with the University as of the start date for the position or the University’s petition being approved and on the candidate being able to obtain the appropriate visa status. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at: In addition to the faculty salary listed below, the four-year Director appointment includes an administrative stipend equal to 7–10% of the faculty base salary. Professor: $120,000 - $130,000 per 9-month academic year. Additional salary for summer months can be sourced from internal funds for additional duties or external funding. Associate Professor: $110,000 - $115,000 per 9-month academic year. Additional salary for summer months can be sourced from internal funds for additional duties or external funding. Teaching Professor-track appointment: $95,000 - $105,000 per 9-month academic year. Additional salary for summer months can be sourced from internal funds for additional duties or external funding. Teaching Associate Professor-track appointment: $85,000 - $95,000 per 9-month academic year. Additional salary for summer months can be sourced from internal funds for additional duties or external funding. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by January 1, 2027. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Interested applicants are welcome to continue to apply. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A one-page cover letter describing interest in the Director of Construction role and relevant qualifications. A current CV/resume. A two-page director philosophy. The names and contact information of three professional references. Candidates will be notified before references are contacted. Questions should be directed to David Mays, David.Mays@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41299&lang=en": {
      "title": "Director of Development SEHD",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/15/2026 Posted Date: Oct 2, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: ADM-CHAN Advancement Denver Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Development Senior Professional Working Title: Director of Development (SEHD) FTE: Full-time 1.0 (100%) FTE | Hybrid Salary Range: $90,000-$97,000 Position #00300024 – Requisition #41299 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . *This is a term limited position that is currently funded through June 30th 2029.* The position will be reviewed by January 2029 to assess financial sustainability. If your term of employment will be concluded based on a lack of funding, you will be provided 30 days’ notice of the term of employment in writing by the University. Director of Development (SEHD) The University of Colorado Denver is a leading public urban research university located in the heart of downtown Denver, where academic excellence, innovation and community partnership come together to expand opportunity and strengthen communities. Within this dynamic institution, the School of Education & Human Development is a statewide and national leader in educational quality, access and equity across the lifespan, from birth through higher education. SEHD prepares equity-minded educators, counselors, researchers and leaders who create meaningful change in schools, families and communities across Colorado and beyond. Through innovative academic programs, impactful research and deep community partnerships, the school advances well-being, opportunity and lifelong learning in diverse urban, rural and global communities. The Director of Development will join SEHD at an important moment of opportunity, building philanthropic partnerships that expand student access, elevate faculty and research, and extend the school’s impact for generations to come. What you will do: Job Purpose: The Director of Development is responsible for leading, developing and executing advancement efforts, which includes developing and managing relationships with gift prospects, and soliciting and stewarding major gifts to maximize philanthropic support for the School of Education & Human Development. Serves as a philanthropic advisor and builds relationships within the School of Education & Human Development to facilitate meaningful connections for donors. Impact & Influence: In a centralized Advancement structure with a team of 23 professionals, the Director of Development is a major gift officer who will grow the pipeline of major and principal gift prospects and donors by partnering with university administration, the Vice Chancellor for Advancement, Executive Director, Deans, faculty, and other campus partners and collaborate with Donor Engagement, Advancement Communications, Alumni Engagement, Gift Planning, fellow major gift officers, and colleagues in central Advancement, including Research, Strategy, and Prospect Management. Success in this role requires strong collaboration, strategic management of donor relationships, accountability for fundraising results, and a commitment to ethical, donor-centered fundraising practices. Key Responsibilities - 80% Maximize the philanthropic potential of a portfolio of major gift prospects by soliciting and closing gifts that advance the mission of the university with a portfolio heavily populated but not limited to Alumni, friends, corporations and foundations. The portfolio will vacillate based on the rating and research analysis of the best targets for major gift opportunities in service to the university. Qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward a portfolio of 80-100 prospects, including individuals, corporations, and foundations (SEHD has a long history of heavy corporate, foundation, and gift planning engagement) for gifts of $25,000+. Align donor interests with the strategic priorities of the university. Analyze prospect research materials to target likely donors of $25,000+. Ensure that contact reports are written and entered in a timely manner and utilized to gain the maximum value from future donor engagements. Work with Executive Director of Development on developing an annual fundraising operating plan to establish fundraising goals built on portfolio projections and historical fundraising. Build fundraising strategies around existing research activities, specifically interdisciplinary societal grand challenges identified by the university. In addition to fundraising goals, the Director of Development will be responsible for meeting specific process targets designed to ensure a disciplined approach to building a successful major gift program, such as visits, proposals, and portfolio penetration. Serve as an Advancement liaison to campus units - 20% Creating and implementing fundraising strategies for programs and initiatives that focus on rated targets of $25,000+ Developing familiarity with campus and unit fundraising priorities Working collaboratively with campus partners and Advancement colleagues to formulate plans for prospect engagement and identifying potential top donors not yet aware of or engaged with the university Facilitating interaction between major gift prospects and campus and system leaders and faculty other duties as assigned. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Bachelor’s Degree in nonprofit management, business, marketing or other related field Two (2) years of experience in fundraising, alumni/donor relations, or other nonprofit role or equivalent experience in for-profit marketing or project management or directly related field Substitution: A combination of education and related technical/paraprofessional experience may be substituted for the bachelor’s degree on a year for year basis. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Master’s Degree in a directly related field. Five (5) or more years of professional related experience in fundraising in a college or university development program or other comparable charitable organization. Experience managing high-level volunteers. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of major gift fundraising within a higher education or similar environment. Superior relationship building skills. Proven success in securing and conducting face to face meetings for major solicitations. Excellent spoken and written communication skills. Ability to prioritize, negotiate, and influence effectively. Effective interpersonal, strategic planning, administrative and organizational skills. Solid judgement and sensitivity to others. Ability to work independently and as part of a larger team. Familiarity with financial planning, tax and charitable gift laws, and planned giving vehicles and methods. Ability to effectively manage multiple assignments simultaneously. Ability to cold call. Ability to qualify and disqualify. Ability to work with a generalized prospect pool. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and internet navigation tools with proven knowledge of word processing, spreadsheet software, email, internet access software, and CRM. Ability to meet metrics and establish goals. Maintain excellent records and stay up to date with contact reports, expense reports, and all required internal administrative processes. Proven track record of donor engagement to include identification, cultivation, solicitation at > $25,000. Ability to create individual donor strategies for solicitation. Demonstrates commitment to ethical fundraising practices and effective engagement with various constituencies. Other Variables: Position is highly visible and requires weekends and evenings for attendance at special events, travel, dinners, athletic events, and entertainment. This position requires great flexibility around weekend and evening activities. Shows enthusiasm for urban public higher education mission. Must have legal driver’s license. Conditions of Employment Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Position is highly visible and requires weekends and evenings for attendance at special events, travel, dinners, athletic events, and entertainment. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $90,000-$97,000. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Oct 16, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: • A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications • A current CV/resume • List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to nickolas.chabot-olson@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41297&lang=en": {
      "title": "Director of Development - CAM",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/15/2026 Posted Date: Oct 2, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: ADM-CHAN Advancement Denver Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Development Senior Professional Working Title: Director of Development (CAM) FTE: Full-time 1.0 (100%) FTE | Hybrid Salary Range: $90,000-$97,000 Position #000001242 – Requisition #41297 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . *This is a term limited position that is currently funded through June 30th 2029.* The position will be reviewed by January 2029 to assess financial sustainability. If your term of employment will be concluded based on a lack of funding, you will be provided 30 days’ notice of the term of employment in writing by the University. Director of Development (CAM) The University of Colorado Denver is Colorado’s public urban research university, located in the heart of a city known for creativity, innovation and cultural vitality. Within this dynamic environment, the College of Arts & Media (CAM) is home to an ambitious community of artists, makers, scholars and innovators preparing to shape the rapidly evolving creative industries. Through nationally recognized programs in film and television, music and entertainment, visual arts, recording arts and media forensics, CAM combines rigorous academic study with hands-on creative practice, state-of-the-art technology and meaningful connections to Denver’s thriving arts and entertainment community. The Director of Development will join CAM at an exciting moment of possibility, building philanthropic partnerships that expand access for talented students, faculty and creative excellence, strengthen industry and community engagement, and bring bold artistic ideas to life. What you will do: Job Purpose: The Director of Development (CAM) is responsible for leading, developing and executing advancement efforts, which includes developing and managing relationships with gift prospects, and soliciting and stewarding major gifts to maximize philanthropic support for the College of Arts & Media. Serves as a philanthropic advisor and builds relationships within the College of Arts & Media to facilitate meaningful connections for donors. Impact & Influence: In a centralized Advancement structure with a team of 23 professionals, the Director of Development is a major gift officer who will grow the pipeline of major and principal gift prospects and donors by partnering with university administration, the Vice Chancellor for Advancement, Executive Director, Deans, faculty, and other campus partners and collaborate with Donor Engagement, Advancement Communications, Alumni Engagement, Gift Planning, fellow major gift officers, and colleagues in central Advancement, including Research, Strategy, and Prospect Management. Success in this role requires strong collaboration, strategic management of donor relationships, accountability for fundraising results, and a commitment to ethical, donor-centered fundraising practices. Key Responsibilities - 80% Maximize the philanthropic potential of a portfolio of major gift prospects by soliciting and closing gifts that advance the mission of the university with a portfolio heavily populated but not limited to Alumni, friends, corporations and foundations. The portfolio will vacillate based on the rating and research analysis of the best targets for major gift opportunities in service to the university. Qualify, cultivate, solicit, and steward a portfolio of 80-100 prospects, including individuals, corporations, and foundations, for gifts of $25,000+. Align donor interests with the strategic priorities of the university. Analyze prospect research materials to target likely donors of $25,000+. Ensure that contact reports are written and entered in a timely manner and utilized to gain the maximum value from future donor engagements. Work with Executive Director on developing an annual fundraising operating plan to establish fundraising goals built on portfolio projections and historical fundraising. Build fundraising strategies around existing research activities, specifically interdisciplinary societal grand challenges identified by the university. In addition to fundraising goals, the Director of Development will be responsible for meeting specific process targets designed to ensure a disciplined approach to building a successful major gift program, such as visits, proposals, and portfolio implementation. Position is highly visible and requires weekends and evenings for attendance at special events, travel, dinners, athletic events, and entertainment. This position requires great flexibility around weekend and evening activities. Serve as an Advancement liaison to campus units - 20% Creating and implementing fundraising strategies for programs and initiatives that focus on rated targets of $25,000+. Developing familiarity with campus and unit fundraising priorities. Working collaboratively with campus partners and Advancement colleagues to formulate plans for prospect engagement and identifying potential top donors not yet aware of or engaged with the university. Facilitating interaction between major gift prospects and campus and system leaders and faculty. Other duties as assigned. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Bachelor’s Degree in nonprofit management, business, marketing or other related field. Two (2) years of experience in fundraising, alumni/donor relations, or other nonprofit role or equivalent experience in for-profit marketing or project management or directly related field. Substitution: A combination of education and related technical/paraprofessional experience may be substituted for the bachelor’s degree on a year for year basis. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Master’s Degree in a directly related field. Five (5) or more years of professional related experience in fundraising in a college or university development program or other comparable charitable organization. Experience managing high-level volunteers. Familiarity with the arts community, including visual and multi-media. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of major gift fundraising within a higher education or similar environment. Superior relationship building skills. . Proven success in securing and conducting face to face meetings for major solicitations . Excellent spoken and written communication skills. Ability to prioritize, negotiate, and influence effectively. Effective interpersonal, strategic planning, administrative and organizational skills. Solid judgement and sensitivity to others. Ability to work independently and as part of a larger team. Familiarity with financial planning, tax and charitable gift laws, and planned giving vehicles and methods. Ability to effectively manage multiple assignments simultaneously. Ability to cold call. Ability to qualify and disqualify. Ability to work with a generalized prospect pool. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications and internet navigation tools with proven knowledge of word processing, spreadsheet software, email, internet access software, and CRM. Ability to meet metrics and establish goals. Maintain excellent records and stay up to date with contact reports, expense reports, and all required internal administrative processes. Proven track record of donor engagement to include identification, cultivation, solicitation of more than $25,000. Ability to create individual donor strategies for solicitation. Demonstrates commitment to ethical fundraising practices and effective engagement with various constituencies. Other Variables: Position is highly visible and requires weekends and evenings for attendance at special events, travel, dinners, athletic events, and entertainment. This position requires great flexibility around weekend and evening activities. Shows enthusiasm for urban public higher education mission. Must have legal driver’s license. Conditions of Employment This is a term limited position that is currently funded through June 30th 2029. Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Position is highly visible and requires weekends and evenings for attendance at special events, travel, dinners, athletic events, and entertainment. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $90,000-$97,000. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by Oct 16, 2026, 11:59:00 PM. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications A current CV/resume List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to nickolas.chabot-olson@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41199&lang=en": {
      "title": "Dean of the Business School",
      "description": "Posted Date: Sep 23, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: VCASA/EVCASA Academic&Stdnt Af Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Dean Working Title: Dean of the Business School FTE: Full-time Salary Range: $260,000 - $320,000 Position # 00350380 – Requisition #41199 About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at Summit Search Solutions- Dean of the Business School - University of Colorado Denver (Denver, CO) * The Dean of the Business School, reporting to the Provost and Executive Vice Chancellor for Academic Affairs, is responsible for serving as the chief academic officer of the school with full responsibility for its academic programs and curricula, the school’s budget, outreach and engagement with the business community, and development activities. In collaboration with the faculty, the Dean will provide intellectual leadership and a strong commitment to the School’s undergraduate and graduate programs. The Dean is expected to increase the school’s resource base by leading fundraising activities for the school, in partnership with an advancement officer. The Dean will also: strengthen engagement of and collaborations with industry and business partners; increase the amount and visibility of scholarship (research/creative work) activity of the faculty; work to promote transformation, innovation and entrepreneurial activities that will prepare the school well for the future; lead the development of strategies to increase enrollments, improve retention and enhance the student experience including internships and other experiential learning activities; foster a climate that demonstrates a commitment to diversity and inclusion; develop a strong alumni network in conjunction with an enhanced focus on job placement; and demonstrate a strong commitment to the university’s urban location while exploring opportunities to engage across Colorado and around the globe. Dean of Business School What you will do: Reporting to the Provost and Executive Vice Chancellor for Academic Affairs, the Dean serves as the chief academic, administrative, and financial officer of the Business School. The Dean provides visionary, collaborative, and entrepreneurial leadership that advances the School's mission and aligns with the university's strategic plan. Working closely with faculty, staff, students, alumni, industry partners, and university leadership, the Dean will strengthen academic excellence, expand the School's impact, foster innovation and entrepreneurship, support research production, and enhance student success and workforce outcomes. Strategic Leadership and Vision Provide bold, collaborative leadership to advance the Business School and position it for long-term success. • Develop and implement a strategic vision for the Business School that aligns with the university's refreshed strategic plan and reflects the opportunities of a leading urban research university. • Foster a culture of innovation, adaptability, and continuous improvement in response to emerging trends in business, technology, workforce development, and higher education. • Partner with faculty, staff, students, alumni, and community stakeholders to identify priorities and achieve measurable outcomes. • Collaborate with the Provost, Deans, and campus leaders to advance institution-wide goals and initiatives. • Serve as the chief advocate and ambassador for the Business School locally, statewide, nationally, and globally. Academic Excellence, Research, Innovation, and Entrepreneurship Advance excellence in teaching, scholarship, innovation, and entrepreneurial thinking across all programs. • Provide intellectual leadership for undergraduate, graduate, executive, and professional education programs. • Support faculty excellence in teaching, research, creative work, and community-engaged scholarship. • Increase the visibility, impact, and external funding of faculty scholarship and research. • Promote interdisciplinary collaboration and partnerships that address complex business and societal challenges. • Strengthen innovation, entrepreneurship, and experiential learning throughout the curriculum and co-curricular experience. • Ensure successful maintenance and continuous improvement of AACSB accreditation and academic quality standards. • Position the School as a leader in emerging areas such as artificial intelligence, data analytics, digital transformation, sustainability, and entrepreneurial innovation. Student Success and Career Outcomes Champion a student-centered culture that promotes access, success, belonging, and career readiness. • Lead strategies that increase enrollment, persistence, retention, graduation, and post-graduation success. • Enhance the student experience through high-impact practices, experiential learning, internships, applied projects, and global learning opportunities. • Strengthen pathways that support student achievement and social mobility. • Expand partnerships that create employment opportunities, career preparation, and professional mentorship. • Foster an inclusive learning environment where all students can thrive and achieve their educational goals. • Utilize data-informed decisionsto support enrollment management, student success, and program effectiveness. Partnerships, Advancement, and External Engagement Cultivate relationships that advance the School's reputation, impact, and resource base. • Build and strengthen strategic partnerships with business, industry, government, nonprofit, and community organizations. • Expand collaborations that support internships, workforce development, sponsored research, innovation, and entrepreneurship. • Lead fundraising efforts in partnership with University Advancement to secure philanthropic support and strategic investments. • Engage alumni as ambassadors, mentors, advisors, employers, and donors. • Enhance the visibility and reputation of the Business School through thought leadership and engagement with external stakeholders. • Leverage the university's urban location to create meaningful regional, statewide, national, and global partnerships. Organizational, Financial, and People Leadership Lead with transparency, collaboration, accountability, and a commitment to shared governance. • Oversee the School's budget, personnel, facilities, and resources to ensure long-term fiscal sustainability. • Recruit, retain, develop, and support outstanding faculty and staff. • Support department chairs and program directors in achieving academic and strategic goals. • Foster a culture of collaboration, accountability, trust, shared governance, and mutual respect. • Promote excellence through clear expectations, assessment, and continuous improvement. • Advance a welcoming and inclusive environment that values diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds. • Meet established goals and performance expectations while ensuring responsible stewardship of university resources. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. • Earned terminal degree in a discipline relevant to the Business School's academic programs. • Record of transformative leadership demonstrating the ability to develop and execute strategic priorities, lead organizational change, and achieve measurable results. • Evidence of success building partnerships with industry, government, nonprofit, and community organizations. • Demonstrated ability to secure resources through fundraising, external partnerships, sponsored programs, or other revenue-generating activities. • Proven ability to lead collaboratively and effectively in complex organizations. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) • Successful senior leadership experience at a research university, business school, or comparable complex organization. • Demonstrated record of advancing innovation, entrepreneurship, and interdisciplinary collaboration. • Experience leading initiatives that improve student success, enrollment growth, retention, and career outcomes. • Record of advancing inclusive excellence and fostering environments where diverse communities thrive. • Understanding of the evolving landscape of business education, including technology-enabled learning, AI, workforce partnerships, and emerging business disciplines. • Experience with strategic planning, accreditation, and organizational transformation. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities • A strong record of thought leadership and influence in business; credibility with business and government leaders as an innovator in business practice and education; and strong mentorship; • Exceptional visionary and strategic thinking and leadership skills; • A record of successful engagement with the business community; • Excellence in leading, evaluating, recruiting and analyzing; • Demonstrated management and administrative ability; • Effectiveness as a fundraiser, networker and strategic alliance-builder; • A commitment to diversity and inclusion; • Successful experience and/or strong potential for success building and/or enhancing strong programs of corporate sponsorships, funded research, and other mission-aligned revenue generation; • Success in establishing mutually beneficial relationships with external constituents such as, but not limited to, alumni, government, and accrediting bodies; • Excellent oral and written communication skills; • The ability to build and work with teams up and across the organization; • Demonstrable accomplishments in working with business-related constituencies that will bridge to a university setting; • A strong record demonstrating a broad working knowledge of innovation and accomplishment, entrepreneurship and technology application in business at the local, state, national and international levels; • Experience with CRM systems and data driven decision making that will translate to enrollment and marketing; • Ability to lead the development of strategies to increase enrollments, improve retention and enhance the student experience including internships and experiential learning activities. Conditions of Employment • Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. • This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. • This position is for a three-year appointment. A review will be conducted during the third year to determine reappointment. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements • The ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $260,000 - $320,000. For candidates considering a move, relocation assistance may be available. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by October 10, 2026. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: Summit Search Solutions- Dean of the Business School - University of Colorado Denver (Denver, CO) and attach: • A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications • A current CV/resume • List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to Lyndi Hewitt, lhewitt@summitsearchsolutions.com Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41193&lang=en": {
      "title": "Dean, School of Public Affairs",
      "description": "Posted Date: Sep 23, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: VCASA/EVCASA Academic&Stdnt Af Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Dean Working Title: Dean, School of Public Affairs FTE: Full Time 1.0 FTE | Hybrid Salary Range: $235,000 - $275,000 Position #00611800– Requisition #41193 Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at: https://www.imsearch.com/open-searches/university-colorado-denver-school-public-affairs/dean Dean, School of Public Affairs Strategic Leadership and Vision Provide bold, collaborative leadership to advance the School of Public Affairs and position it for long-term success. • Develop and implement a strategic vision for the School of Public Affairs that aligns with the university's refreshed strategic plan and reflects the opportunities of a leading urban research university. • Foster a culture of innovation, adaptability, and continuous improvement in response to emerging issues in public policy, public administration, social equity, governance, nonprofit leadership, criminal justice, public finance, and civic engagement. • Partner with faculty, staff, students, alumni, and community stakeholders to identify priorities and achieve measurable outcomes. • Collaborate with the Provost, Deans, and campus leaders to advance institution-wide goals and initiatives. • Serve as the chief advocate and ambassador for the School of Public Affairs locally, statewide, nationally, and globally. • Position the School as a thought leader in addressing complex public challenges facing cities, states, nations, and communities. Academic Excellence, Research, and Public Impact Advance excellence in teaching, scholarship, policy engagement, and public service. • Provide intellectual leadership for undergraduate, graduate, and professional education programs. • Support faculty excellence in teaching, research, creative work, policy engagement, and community-engaged scholarship. • Increase the visibility, impact, and external funding of faculty scholarship and research. • Promote interdisciplinary collaboration and partnerships that address pressing public challenges and inform public decision-making. • Support applied research, policy analysis, and evidence-based solutions that contribute to the public good. • Ensure successful maintenance and continuous improvement of NASPAA accreditation and academic quality standards. • Strengthen the School's national reputation and ranking as a leading school of public affairs. • Advance scholarship and educational programs in areas such as public policy, public administration, nonprofit management, criminal justice, public budgeting and finance, sustainability, governance, technology and society, public health policy, and social equity. • Foster partnerships among faculty, research centers, policymakers, and practitioners to translate knowledge into action and public impact. Student Success and Leadership Development Champion a student-centered culture that promotes access, success, belonging, and preparation for public leadership. • Lead strategies that increase enrollment, persistence, retention, graduation, and post-graduation success. • Prepare students for impactful careers across government, nonprofit, philanthropic, international, and public-serving organizations. • Enhance the student experience through experiential learning, internships, capstone projects, community-engaged learning, and public service opportunities. • Strengthen pathways that support student achievement, leadership development, and social mobility. • Expand partnerships that create employment opportunities, policy fellowships, public service experiences, and professional mentorship. • Foster an inclusive learning environment where all students can thrive and achieve their educational goals. • Utilize data-informed decisions to support enrollment management, student success, and program effectiveness. • Promote civic engagement, ethical leadership, and a lifelong commitment to public service. Partnerships, Advancement, and External Engagement Cultivate relationships that advance the School's reputation, impact, and resource base. • Build and strengthen strategic partnerships with local, state, federal, and international government agencies; nonprofit organizations; philanthropic foundations; community organizations; and private-sector partners. • Expand collaborations that support research, policy innovation, experiential learning, workforce development, and community impact. • Serve as a visible and trusted leader in policy, civic, and community conversations that advance the public good. • Lead fundraising efforts in partnership with University Advancement to secure philanthropic support and strategic investments. • Engage alumni as ambassadors, mentors, advisors, practitioners, and donors. • Enhance the visibility and reputation of the School through thought leadership, public engagement, and professional association involvement. • Leverage the university's urban location and proximity to the seat of state government to create meaningful regional, statewide, national, and global partnerships. • Support executive education, professional development, and community-facing programs that serve public sector and nonprofit leaders. Organizational, Financial, and People Leadership Lead with transparency, collaboration, accountability, and a commitment to shared governance. • Oversee the School's budget, personnel, facilities, and resources to ensure long-term fiscal sustainability. • Recruit, retain, develop, and support outstanding faculty and staff. • Support center directors and program leaders in achieving academic, research, and strategic goals. • Foster a culture of collaboration, accountability, trust, shared governance, and mutual respect. • Promote excellence through clear expectations, assessment, and continuous improvement. • Advance a welcoming and inclusive environment that values diverse perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds. • Support faculty and staff development while fostering a culture of engagement, innovation, and high performance. • Meet established goals and performance expectations while ensuring responsible stewardship of university resources. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. • A terminal degree in a relevant discipline offered by the School of Public Affairs, from an accredited institution. • Record of transformative leadership demonstrating the ability to develop and execute strategic priorities, lead organizational change, and achieve measurable results. • Evidence of success building partnerships with government, nonprofit, and community organizations. • Demonstrated ability to successfully secure resources through fundraising, external partnerships, sponsored programs, or other revenue-generating activities. • Proven ability to lead collaboratively and effectively in complex organizations. • Substantial financial management experience, including the equitable allocation of resources across multiple programs and initiatives, and supporting innovation around new degrees, certificates, or other revenue generators. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) • Successful senior leadership experience at a research university, school of public affairs, or comparable complex organization. • Demonstrated record of advancing innovation and interdisciplinary collaboration. • Experience leading initiatives that improve student success, enrollment growth, retention, and career outcomes. • An innovative, intellectually engaged leader who makes difficult decisions with reflection, fairness, and transparency and is willing to challenge the status quo to guide the college to further prominence locally, nationally, and internationally. • A clear, transparent, empathetic, and empowering communication style; an openness to listening and integrating the input of others; an ability to advocate for the college's priorities; an ability to convey the mission, vision, and impact of the college and university to both internal and external audiences; and the capacity to serve as a thought leader within Colorado and beyond. • A champion for inclusive excellence, access and campus engagement with exemplary skills in communicating, collaborating with, and supporting a diverse community, removing barriers to inclusion, promoting student voice, and valuing student agency. • Ability to foster a deep sense of community both internally at the college and externally with partners; desire to listen and learn and to serve as a visible and positive presence at the school and on campus; and ability to foster a culture and community where kindness, humility, and respect are high priorities. • A relationship-based leadership style that values teamwork and collaboration; a firm belief in shared governance and a high level of collegiality in working with faculty, staff, and students; experience bringing people together to solve challenges and seize opportunities; ability to delegate; respect and appreciation for all of the disciplines of the college; and experience facilitating strong partnerships among academic programs and other divisions of the university. • A leadership style that demonstrates accountability, integrity, compassion, empathy, kindness, humility, curiosity, enthusiasm, humor, flexibility, forward-thinking, good judgment, and discernment. • A deep appreciation for research and scholarly endeavors, a record of recruiting and developing both people and programs of the highest quality. • Successful experience in or a demonstrated capacity for fundraising; an eagerness to build relationships with donors, alumni, and community partnerships to help advance the mission of SPA. • Familiarity with the issues and opportunities across a range of academic areas and a natural curiosity about a broad range of intellectual endeavors, including those represented throughout the college’s programs. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities • Credibility with community and government leaders as a thought leader and innovator in industry practice and education Exceptional visionary and strategic thinking and leadership skills. • Excellence in leading, evaluating, recruiting and analyzing. • Demonstrated management and administrative ability. • Effectiveness as a fundraiser, networker and strategic alliance-builder. • Strong, demonstrated commitment and accomplishment in inclusive excellence, access and campus engagement. • Successful experience and/or strong potential for success building and/or enhancing strong programs of corporate sponsorships, funded research, and other mission-aligned revenue generation. • Success in establishing mutually beneficial relationships with internal and external constituents such as, but not limited to, alumni, government, and accrediting bodies. • Excellent oral and written communication skills. • The ability to build and work with teams up and across the organization. • The ability to resolve conflict in a positive manner. • Demonstrable accomplishments in working with a variety of teams, groups, and leaders that will bridge to a university setting. • Experience with data driven decision making that translates to enrollment and marketing. • Ability to lead the development of strategies to increase enrollments, improve retention and enhance the student experience including internships and experiential learning activities. Conditions of Employment • Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. • This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. • This position is for a three-year appointment. A review will be conducted during the third year to determine reappointment. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements • The ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $235,000 - $275,000 The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline • Applications will be accepted until finalists are identified, and interviews begin. Preference will be given to complete applications received by October 5th, 2026. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: https://www.imsearch.com/open-searches/university-colorado-denver-school-public-affairs/dean Questions should be directed to Janette Martinez, jmartinez@imsearch.com Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41101&lang=en": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted Date: Sep 15, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CEDC-Computer SC & Engineering Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Assistant Professor Working Title: Assistant Professor FTE: Full-time Salary Range: $90,000 - $125,000 Position #00636050– Requisition #41101 About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Computer Science and Engineering Department (CSE) currently has sixteen full-time faculty members. The department offers a ABET accredited Bachelor of Science degree in Computer Science, a Bachelor of Arts degree in Computer Science, a Bachelor of Science degree in Cybersecurity, a Master of Science degree in Computer Science, and two multidisciplinary PhD degrees in Computer Science and Information Systems and Engineering and Applied Science, and several certificates. The CSE department provides quality computer science education and research opportunities to our students and quality professional service to the professional community and the general public in the Denver metropolitan area. The current strengths of the faculty are in the areas of algorithms, artificial intelligence, computer networks, graphics, parallel and distributed systems, mobile systems, machine learning, Cybersecurity and secure computing, data science and AI, and theory. Our faculty are engaged in multidisciplinary research and encourage collaborative research among disciplines. In addition to general computing labs, the department houses several specialized research labs in Next Generation Networking & Security, Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS), Active Cyber Infrastructure and Defense (ACID), Machine Learning (ML), networks (DECENT), Big Data Management & Mining Laboratory (BDLab), Animation, Neuroscience & AI, and parallel and distributed systems (PDS). The College of Engineering, Design and Computing values innovation, creativity and an interdisciplinary approach to engineering and research. We focus on providing our students with a comprehensive engineering education at the undergraduate, graduate and professional level. Faculty conduct research that spans our five disciplines of civil, electrical and mechanical engineering, bioengineering, and computer science and engineering. The college collaborates with industry from around the state; our laboratories and research opportunities give students the hands-on experience they need to excel in the professional world. Assistant Professor What you will do: The Department of Computer Science and Engineering in the College of Engineering, Design and Computing at the University of Colorado Denver invites applications for Assistant Professor. Reporting to the Chair of the Computer Science and Engineering Department, these positions will be expected to develop and teach lecture and laboratory courses at all levels; establish an active externally funded research program; conduct high quality research involving students at all levels, leading to sponsored research and refereed publications; advise students; and contribute to Department, College, and University governance and to the profession. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. • Ph.D. in Computer Science or closely related field. • Demonstrated expertise and track record of research and teaching accomplishments commensurate with the candidate’s rank in computer science, as evidenced by their record. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) • While candidates in all areas of computer science will be considered, the areas of AI, Quantum Computing (including Quantum Machine Learning), Health Informatics and Technologies, Intelligent Systems, Cybersecurity, Edge Computing, Computer Vision and Cyber-Physical Systems, and/or a related field will be given priority. Conditions of Employment • These positions may follow a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preference. Working from the office is required for teaching, student interactions, committee work, and other responsibilities. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements • These positions may be performed on site, and will require the ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. • These positions may be performed in a hybrid or remote environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position may be eligible now and in the future for certain visa sponsorship limited to Change of Status (COS) within the United States or if the candidate meets the criteria for the portability provision. While the hiring unit may be willing to pursue certain visa sponsorship, an offer of employment will be contingent on the candidate having a visa status that allows employment with the University as of the start date for the position or the University’s petition being approved and on the candidate being able to obtain the appropriate visa status. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at: $90,000 - $125,000. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline • Applications will be accepted until finalists are identified, and interviews begin. Preference will be given to complete applications received by November 27, 2026. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: • Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). • One (1) PDF containing the following documents combined (may not exceed 2.5 megabytes): o A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. o Statement of teaching philosophy, experience, and interests (3-page limit). o Statement of research philosophy, experience, and interests (3-page limit). o List of three professional references including name, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting references). Questions should be directed to Kelly Konrad, kelly.p.konrad@ucdenver.edu Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41047&lang=en": {
      "title": "Crisis Counseling Manager",
      "description": "Posted Date: Sep 9, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: VCSSLS-HWAS Stdnt&CommCounslng Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Health Care Manager Working Title: Crisis Counseling Manager FTE: Full-time Salary Range: $84,206 - $93,070 Position #00691789 – Requisition #41047 About the University of Colorado - Denver The University of Colorado Denver is the state’s premier public urban research university and equity-serving institution. Globally connected and locally invested, CU Denver partners with future-focused learners and communities to design accessible, relevant, transformative educational experiences for every stage of life and career. Across seven schools and colleges in the heart of downtown Denver, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges through break through innovations and impactful research, and creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $800 million. For more information, visit ucdenver.edu. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Crisis Counseling Manager is a licensed mental health professional within the CU Denver Student and Community Counseling Center and reports to the Assistant Director at the Counseling Center. This position provides leadership for the Counseling Center's crisis services program and is responsible for ensuring timely, coordinated, and effective responses to students experiencing acute mental health concerns and elevated levels of risk. The Crisis Counseling Manager oversees the assessment, triage, case coordination, and follow-up of high-risk students while maintaining continuity of care across university and community systems. This role collaborates closely with campus partners including Disability Resources and Services, Student Conduct and Community Standards, the Office of Case Management, faculty, International Student Services, the Ombuds Office, Auraria Health Center, and other stakeholders to support student wellbeing and safety. The Crisis Counseling Manager develops and maintains crisis response protocols, risk management procedures, referral pathways, and systems for tracking and coordinating care for students identified as high risk. The position facilitates transitions to higher levels of care when student needs exceed the Counseling Center's scope of practice and supports successful reintegration to campus following hospitalization, medical leave, or other significant mental health events. The Crisis Counseling Manager provides consultation to students, families, faculty, staff, administrators, and community providers regarding complex mental health concerns and crisis response. In addition to program leadership responsibilities, the Crisis Counseling Manager provides culturally responsive clinical services, including crisis intervention, brief individual counseling, group counseling, case management, intake assessment, consultation, outreach, and prevention programming. The position also develops and delivers training and educational programs for campus stakeholders regarding student mental health, crisis response, and wellbeing promotion. The Crisis Counseling Manager supervises crisis counseling staff, contributes to the training and development of clinical trainees, and participates in departmental meetings, university committees, and collaborative initiatives that support student success and wellbeing. Based on a total of 100% of time, this position will be responsible for approximately: 40% Coordination of Crisis Services 30% Counseling Services (includes on-call) 10% Administrative (e.g., staff meeting, team meeting, trainee review, high-risk) 15% supervision and practicum student training 5% outreach and prevention services Crisis Counseling Manager What you will do: • Lead and coordinate the Counseling Center's crisis services program, including policy and procedure development, service design, workflow management, staffing plans, clinician coverage, and continuous quality improvement initiatives. • Provide clinical and administrative supervision, performance management, consultation, and professional development for crisis counseling staff. • Oversee training activities related to crisis counseling for clinical staff, trainees, and practicum students. • Provide drop-in crisis assessment and intervention services; coordinate care for students experiencing acute distress through consultation, case management, collaboration with campus partners, and referral to community resources when appropriate. • Develop and maintain systems for triage, referral management, case tracking, documentation, and follow-up of students identified as high risk. • Consult with students, families, faculty, staff, administrators, and community providers regarding complex mental health concerns, crisis response, hospitalization follow-up, and return-to-campus planning. • Provide culturally responsive brief counseling, crisis intervention, group counseling, case management, and outreach services to a diverse student population. • Monitor and evaluate crisis services utilization, outcomes, and operational effectiveness; use data to inform program improvements and resource planning. • Represent the Counseling Center on multidisciplinary teams addressing students of concern, behavioral risk, and university crisis response efforts. • Participate in recruitment, hiring, onboarding, and training of crisis services staff. • Develop and deliver trainings, workshops, and consultations for campus stakeholders on mental health, crisis response, and student wellbeing; participate in departmental meetings, committees, and outreach initiatives. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) • Master’s degree in counseling-related field from an accredited institution. • Licensure as mental health professional in Colorado (LPC, LMFT, LCSW). • At least three (3) years of related professional level experience Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) • Experience with crisis management, including assessment, intervention, stabilization, and disposition planning. • Experience coordinating care for individuals requiring involuntary mental health evaluation, hospitalization, or higher levels of care, including related assessment, consultation, coordination, and documentation. • History of work in acute settings, such as crisis phone lines, hospital settings, behavioral health/primary care, inpatient treatment facilities, drop-in crisis centers, crisis stabilization units, etc. • Experience providing clinical supervision, consultation, or support to mental health professionals and trainees. • Experience coordinating, developing, or managing clinical programs or services. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities • Ability to communicate effectively, both orally and in writing. • Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships across the institution. • Demonstrated commitment to equitable, inclusive, and culturally responsive clinical practice. • Ability to provide culturally responsive crisis intervention and brief therapy to a diverse student population. • Ability to assess, manage, and respond effectively to students experiencing acute mental health crises. • Ability to conduct suicide, homicide, and behavioral threat risk assessments. • Ability to provide rapid assessment, triage, stabilization, and referral services in a fast-paced clinical environment. • Ability to collaborate effectively within a multidisciplinary treatment team. • Ability to consult with campus and community partners regarding students of concern. • Ability to coordinate care across campus and community systems. • Ability to provide clinical and operational leadership for a crisis services program, including team coordination, workflow management, staff support, program development, and continuous quality improvement. Conditions of Employment • Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. • This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements • This position may be performed in a hybrid or remote environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $84,206 - $93,070. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by priority deadline of September 27th, 2026. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: • A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications • A current CV/resume • List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to Dr. Matthew Heermann, matthew.heermann@ucdenver.edu Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41040&lang=en": {
      "title": "Lecturer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/08/2026 Posted Date: Sep 9, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CLAS-Political Science Schedule: Part-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Lecturer Working Title: Lecturer FTE : Part-time Salary Range: The starting salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established as $1,704 per credit hour (undergraduate level), or $1,849 per credit hour (graduate level). Position #00007258– Requisition #41040 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado - Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top ranked bachelors, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $800 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * This posting will establish and maintain a pool of part-time lecturers with experience teaching Political Science courses in designated fields of the discipline, which may include specializations in areas such as Area Studies, American Political Thought, Research Methodology, Constitutional Law, Environmental Politics, Labor, Social Economy, Local Government Leadership, Community Organizing/Development, and courses within their area of expertise, for the Department of Political Science in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Colorado Denver. The Center for New Directions in Politics & Policy is within the Department of Political Science and manages an applied Master's Degree and several undergraduate and graduate certificates, delivered primarily through weekend intensive in-person graduate courses. New Directions will also hire lecturers from this pool of applicants. By submitting your application, you will become part of an applicant pool. Candidates hired from this pool will fill temporary, non-tenure track positions and will teach no more than two courses per semester. Appointments will be part-time (less than 50%) and will be made semester-by-semester, depending on the needs of the department and subject to budgetary approval. Therefore, this posting does not necessarily reflect a current open position. The Department of Political Science will contact you if there is an opening that suits your qualifications. Working Faculty Title What you will do: Teach one or two courses in the Political Science Department. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Master’s Degree in political Science or related field. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Discipline related certifications PhD or ABD in Political Science. Experience teaching in a higher education environment in related field to teaching assignment. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Expertise in one or more of the related teaching assignment(s). Ability to communicate in a positive and effective manner, across various modalities. Ability to effectively listen and share relevant information. Ability to establish and respond to, as well as, balance multiple work priorities, follow through, and meet established deadlines. Intentionally contribute to a respectful, equitable, positive, and professional workplace. Commitment to enhancing diversity, equity and inclusion. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with employees at all levels throughout the institution. Understanding of the needs of a diverse student population. Strong commitment to student education and success. Experience in online or hybrid delivery. Conditions of Employment Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The starting salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established as $1,704 per credit hour (undergraduate level), or $1,849 per credit hour (graduate level). The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications for this pooled position will be accepted on a rolling basis for up to three (3) months from the posting date [Sept. 9, 2026]. Qualified applicants may be reviewed and approved for hire at any point during this period. Early application is encouraged, as the pool may close before the six‑month window if all openings are filled. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. Please detail your availability in your letter, including the specific days (Monday-Friday, weekends) and times (daytime or evening) you are able to teach. Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). Five professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). Questions should be directed to Kayla Spencer, kayla.spencer@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41035&lang=en": {
      "title": "Instructor - ICB",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/31/2026 Posted Date: Sep 9, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CLAS-Communication Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Instructor Working Title: Instructor - ICB FTE: Full-time, 0.9 (90%) FTE | On Site in Bejing, China Salary Range : $54,000 - $56,692 Position #00637374– Requisition #41035 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado - Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top ranked bachelors, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $800 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Located in the Haidian District of Beijing, International College Beijing (ICB) is an international partnership between China Agricultural University (CAU) and the University of Colorado Denver that offers complete undergraduate programs in Economics and Communication. All courses are administered and taught by CU Denver faculty in English, and the degree earned is awarded by CU Denver. The ICB program offers a thriving and collegial environment that strives to embody the best promises of international education. For more information about the CU Denver/ICB program, please see the International College Beijing web site: https://www.ucdenver.edu/offices/international-affairs/partners/international-college-beijing. What this position offers: initial airline ticket to Beijing is covered by the university and a $2,500 moving allowance is provided, payable once you start. After the initial ticket, an additional allowance is provided to purchase tickets to and from Beijing. Employees receive generous health benefits through Blue Cross Blue Shield with access to top private, English-speaking clinics in Beijing and minimal out-of-pocket costs. U.S. citizens may further qualify for a 100% return of all paid state and federal income taxes. Housing options include free guesthouse apartments (inclusive of all utilities) with private rooms, private showers, shared laundry and kitchen, as well as a gym; or heavily subsidized off-campus housing (within 200ft of campus) that offers spacious 3-bedroom, 2-bath apartments, with a sunroom and city views at higher levels of accommodation. All housing has free maintenance and upkeep. China Agricultural University, whose campus ICB is located on, is nationally famous for its outstanding food offerings – this includes multiple canteens and food vendors across campus that offer highly subsidized, delicious and nutritious food, including a Halal restaurant. At ICB, semesters are 12 to 14 weeks long; semester start dates are based on the lunar calendar with fall semesters beginning early to mid-September, and spring semesters beginning mid-February to early March. Summar and Winter semester teaching may be available for additional pay. Communication courses are offered Mon-Thurs, with no classes on Fridays, which offers opportunities to explore Beijing and its surroundings. As an ancient and modern city, Beijing offers all the amenities of a major U.S. or international city. It has excellent public transportation (including subway, highspeed rail, low-cost taxis, and two major airports) that make getting around town and travel convenient. ICB also provides faculty with professional development funds towards research and/or pedagogy, and offers free Chinese language courses. In compliance with federal law, all persons hired will be required to verify identity and eligibility to work in the United States and to complete the required employment eligibility verification document form upon hire. The University does not have direct authority to hire Chinese citizens working in China. Faculty hired to teach at ICB must be able to meet the requirements to obtain the Z Visa to work in China ( http://english.gov.cn/services/workinchina/ ). Our understanding of these requirements includes: prior to the position start date, international (non-Chinese) candidates must hold their Master’s degree or above and have worked full-time for at least two years in a relevant area after receiving their Bachelor’s degree, or begin their appointment at ICB within 12 months of obtaining their Ph.D. Under these Chinese rules, the maximum age of candidates seeking a work Visa is 59. These requirements are subject to change by the Chinese government without notice. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The University of Colorado Denver Department of Communication invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track teaching position at the International College of Beijing (ICB). The job will commence in Beijing in Spring 2027. Depending on the performance of the individual hired and other circumstances, multi-year renewals are possible. We seek to hire a colleague who can teach a wide array of Communication courses across Health Communication, Digital Media, and humanistic approaches to AI, supporting one or more of the Department’s pathways and certificates, especially in health communication, strategic communication, and/or AI and human communication. This may include courses such as Interpersonal Communication, Communication Research & Writing, Communication & Citizenship, Health Communication, and Digital Health Narratives, among others. We are particularly interested in a colleague who is well-versed in incorporating the use of AI tools and/or critically examines AI’s broader societal impacts. We similarly seek a colleague who is experienced in integrating workplace readiness elements into their course design to prepare students for the workforce. The teaching load is 8 courses per academic year (4 in fall and 4 in spring) and will also assume 10% of their duties in service roles assigned in consultation with the ICB Director, Department Chair, and Associate Program Chair. These service roles may include advising students on the communication major, minor, and certificates; serving as faculty advisor or director of student clubs; contributing to or overseeing annual student festivals and ceremonies; contributing to newsletters and outcomes assessment; and other tasks. Instructor - ICB What you will do: Teach 8 courses per academic year (4 in fall and 4 in spring) Service duties assigned in consultation with the Department Chair, ICB Director, and Associate Program Chair Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Master’s degree in Communication or a closely allied field (English-medium) Two years independent teaching experience as the instructor of record (English-medium) Evidence of highly effective teaching Evidence of multifaceted and excellent service to an academic department Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Ph.D. in Communication (or a closely allied discipline) Demonstrated experience in communication in U.S.-China contexts Demonstrated experience in incorporating AI tools in student assignments and assessments Demonstrated experience integrating workplace readiness and/or career preparation elements into student assignments and assessments Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Proficient skills in instructional technology, digital and social media environments, as well as AI/media production tools (for example, Canvas, ChatGPT, DeepSeek, Adobe, PowerPoint, Canva, and others) Ability to communicate effectively, both in writing and orally, in a variety of institutional contexts. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with students and employees at all levels throughout the institution. Ability to live and work in Beijing as a CU Denver employee Conditions of Employment Required to work in China for this position. Faculty hired to teach at ICB must be able to meet the requirements to obtain the Z Visa to work in China (http://english.gov.cn/services/workinchina/) To be paid via CU Denver payroll, you must have a social security number and a U.S. bank account for direct deposit CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $54,000 - $56,692. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until finalists are identified, and interviews begin. Preference will be given to complete applications received by September 20, 2026. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). Three professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). A single PDF file providing evidence for (a) at least two years independent teaching experience as the instructor of record (English-medium), (b) highly effective teaching, and (c) multifaceted and excellent service to an academic department. Questions should be directed to Dr. Mia Fischer, Associate Professor, Department of Communication, CU Denver, mia.fischer@ucdenver.ed u. Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=39037&lang=en": {
      "title": "Lecturer Pool Posting, School of Education and Human Development",
      "description": "Posted Date: Sep 3, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: SEHD DEAN Schedule: Part-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Lecturer Pool Posting, School of Education and Human Development Working Title: Lecturer Pool Posting, School of Education and Human Development FTE: part-time Salary Range: $5,125 for a fully-enrolled 3-credit course. Position #00626465 Requisition #39037 About the University of Colorado - Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top ranked bachelors, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $800 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The School of Education & Human Development at the University of Colorado Denver is a statewide and national leader for educational quality, access and equity across the education lifespan, from birth through higher education. Members of the school community have been informing policy and practice while addressing the most important challenges faced by individuals, schools, and communities for more than 50 years. We prepare inclusive educational, community, family, and mental health practitioners, scholars, and leaders dedicated to championing change that leads to well-being, opportunity, and life-long learning in the diverse urban, rural, and global communities that we serve. Our Inclusive Excellence Plan reflects our efforts to ensure that those we serve have access to an excellent education through inclusive practice and scholarship in a climate that is responsive to their needs. The school’s degree programs engage students in a strategic and interrelated combination of course work and internships in schools, agencies, and community-based organizations. The school offers three doctoral level programs, an educational specialist degree, 13 master's degrees in multiple program areas, and two undergraduate degrees, along with many licenses, endorsements and certificates. The undergraduate and graduate programs prepare and inspire education and mental health leaders to have a profound impact on fostering opportunity achievement, and success across rural, urban, and diverse communities. Programs are designed to be highly impactful for working professionals and distance learners with evening, online and hybrid class options. Community-engaged and global partnerships are the heartbeat of SEHD. Innovative initiatives co-constructed with school districts, community colleges, and service organizations draw upon personnel strengths in education, human well-being, and equity-centered and community-focused research. These partnerships foster and celebrate diverse community voices, relevant student learning opportunities that build careers, positive outcomes for partner institutions, and outstanding research publications cited around the world. In 2024, SEHD was recognized as the number one graduate online education program in Colorado and among the top graduate education programs in the nation according to U.S. News and World Report. The School of Education & Human Development's strategic plan outlines its commitment to academic excellence, inclusive practices, and community engagement. It emphasizes fostering innovation in teaching, research, and service to meet the evolving needs of students and society. To read the full statement, click here: https://education.ucdenver.edu/about-us/strategic-plan Lecturer The purpose of this posting is to expand our pool of Lecturers available to teach courses for the School of Education & Human Development. Lecturers selected from this pool will become vital members of the School’s innovative education programs. For information on the specific programs and courses offered, please visit https://education.ucdenver.edu. The School of Education & Human Development will establish and maintain a pool of Lecturers for future appointments to temporary, part-time, non-tenure-track positions. Lecturers are generally limited to two courses per term. This will be a semester-by-semester appointment, with the possibility of renewal. As this is a pool posting, multiple hires will be made, and the posting will reopen periodically throughout the year to support semester-based hiring. It is typically open during the following timeframes: mid-February through April, June through July, and September through October. You are welcome to reapply to the new posting at the beginning of each year. Teaching assignments may fluctuate and are contingent upon the schedule of courses offered each term, the required number of student enrollments in your tentatively scheduled course(s), and budget availability. Thus, we cannot guarantee that you will teach particular courses or that you will actually teach in a given term. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. • Significant successful teaching or work experience in education, community, or business contexts, especially in urban and diverse settings. • At a minimum, an earned master’s degree in relevant content area (some courses may require a minimum of an earned doctoral degree). See minimum qualifications for each academic level below. Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Undergraduate Lecturer Minimum Qualifications • M.A. or M.S. in Education, Human Development, Family Relations, or a closely related discipline, or • B.A. or B.S. in Education, Human Development, Family Relations, or a closely related discipline, AND a M.A. or M.S. in any field in addition to at least 18 graduate level credit hours in Education or a closely related discipline, or • If the Master’s Degree is in an unrelated field, the candidate must have a minimum of 5 years of successful experience in the field related to the course documented on the CV. Candidates need to supply some evidence (e.g., subject matter expertise or leadership expertise) of experience. Graduate Lecturer Minimum Qualifications MA Degree Programs • PhD, EdD, PsyD in Education, Counseling, Human Development, Family Relations, or closely related disciple field area. • If the terminal degree is in an unrelated field, the candidate must demonstrate experience in the field related to the course documented on the CV Candidates need to supply some evidence of experience (e.g., subject matter expertise or leadership expertise). • If the candidate does not have a terminal degree, the candidate must have a M.A. or M.S. AND a minimum of 5 years of successful experience in the field related to the course documented on the CV. Candidates need to supply some evidence (e.g., teaching experience, school principal, board certified counselor, etc.) of experience PhD, EdD, PsyD Degree Programs • PhD, EdD, PsyD in Education, Counseling, Human Development, Family Relations, or closely related disciple field area. • If the doctorate degree is in an unrelated field, the candidate must demonstrate at least 18 graduate level credit hours in related discipline and/or 5 years’ experience in the field related to the course documented on the CV. Candidates need to supply some evidence (e.g., subject matter expertise or leadership expertise) of experience. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) • Ed.D. or Ph.D. in Education or a closely related field. • Experience teaching at the graduate level. • Experience teaching online or with blended/hybrid models (e.g. Blackboard or eCollege and LiveText) Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities • Experience with culturally, linguistically, and economically diverse communities, knowledge of urban educational community issues, and an interest in collaborating with a variety of constituencies in an urban environment. • Proficient with basic technology (e.g. MS Office) Conditions of Employment This position involves working closely with students where the person may become aware of campus safety issues that may need to be reported as part of CU’s commitment to compliance with the Clery Act. The person in this role will receive the related training and be required to submit data once per year to the campus Clery Act team. For more information about our commitment to campus safety as it relates to this law, please visit https://www.ucdenver.edu/emergencymanagement/clery-act CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $5,125 for a fully-enrolled 3-credit course. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. As this is a pool posting, multiple hires will be made, and the posting will reopen periodically throughout the year to support semester-based hiring. It is typically open during the following timeframes: mid-February through April, June through July, and September through October. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: • A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. • Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). • Five professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). • Additional materials can be added here if applicable. Questions should be directed to SEHD HR, sehdhr@ucdenver.edu Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=39036&lang=en": {
      "title": "Lecturer - CPE, Continuing and Professional Education (Pool Posting)",
      "description": "Posted Date: Aug 24, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: SEHD CPE Schedule: Part-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Lecturer - CPE, Continuing and Professional Education (Pool Posting) Working Title: Lecturer - CPE, Continuing and Professional Education (Pool Posting) FTE: Part-time Salary Range: $5,125 for a fully-enrolled 3-credit course. Position #00007000 Requisition #39036 About the University of Colorado - Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top ranked bachelors, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $800 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The School of Education & Human Development at the University of Colorado Denver is a statewide and national leader for educational quality, access and equity across the education lifespan, from birth through higher education. Members of the school community have been informing policy and practice while addressing the most important challenges faced by individuals, schools, and communities for more than 50 years. We prepare inclusive educational, community, family, and mental health practitioners, scholars, and leaders dedicated to championing change that leads to well-being, opportunity, and life-long learning in the diverse urban, rural, and global communities that we serve. Our Inclusive Excellence Plan reflects our efforts to ensure that those we serve have access to an excellent education through inclusive practice and scholarship in a climate that is responsive to their needs. The school’s degree programs engage students in a strategic and interrelated combination of course work and internships in schools, agencies, and community-based organizations. The school offers three doctoral level programs, an educational specialist degree, 13 master's degrees in multiple program areas, and two undergraduate degrees, along with many licenses, endorsements and certificates. The undergraduate and graduate programs prepare and inspire education and mental health leaders to have a profound impact on fostering opportunity achievement, and success across rural, urban, and diverse communities. Programs are designed to be highly impactful for working professionals and distance learners with evening, online and hybrid class options. Community-engaged and global partnerships are the heartbeat of SEHD. Innovative initiatives co-constructed with school districts, community colleges, and service organizations draw upon personnel strengths in education, human well-being, and equity-centered and community-focused research. These partnerships foster and celebrate diverse community voices, relevant student learning opportunities that build careers, positive outcomes for partner institutions, and outstanding research publications cited around the world. In 2024, SEHD was recognized as the number one graduate online education program in Colorado and among the top graduate education programs in the nation according to U.S. News and World Report. The School of Education & Human Development's strategic plan outlines its commitment to academic excellence, inclusive practices, and community engagement. It emphasizes fostering innovation in teaching, research, and service to meet the evolving needs of students and society. To read the full statement, click here: https://education.ucdenver.edu/about-us/strategic-plan Lecturer - CPE, Continuing and Professional Education (Pool Posting) What you will do: Our Office of Continuing and Professional Education (CPE) provides a wide array of courses, an alternative teacher licensure program, certificates, endorsements, workshops and leadership training tailored to meet your professional development needs. The purpose of this posting is to expand our pool of Lecturers available to teach courses for CPE. Lecturers selected from this pool will become vital members of the School’s innovative education programs. For information on the specific programs and courses offered through CPE, please visit https://education.ucdenver.edu/continuing-education. The School of Education & Human Development will establish and maintain a pool of Lecturers for future appointments to temporary, part-time, non-tenure-track positions in Continuing and Professional Education (CPE). Lecturers are generally limited to two courses per term. This will be a semester-by-semester appointment, with the possibility of renewal. Teaching assignments may fluctuate and are contingent upon the schedule of courses offered each term, the required number of student enrollments in your tentatively scheduled course(s), and budget availability. Thus, we cannot guarantee that you will teach particular courses or that you will actually teach in a given term. This position involves working closely with students where the person may become aware of campus safety issues that may need to be reported as part of CU’s commitment to compliance with the Clery Act. The person in this role will receive the related training and be required to submit data once per year to the campus Clery Act team. For more information about our commitment to campus safety as it relates to this law, please visit https://www.ucdenver.edu/emergencymanagement/clery-act Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. • Significant successful teaching or work experience in education, community, or business contexts, especially in urban and diverse settings. • At a minimum, an earned master’s degree in relevant content area (some courses may require a minimum of an earned doctoral degree). See minimum qualifications for each academic level below. Undergraduate Lecturer Minimum Qualifications • M.A. or M.S. in Education, Human Development, Family Relations, or a closely related discipline, or • B.A. or B.S. in Education, Human Development, Family Relations, or a closely related discipline, AND a M.A. or M.S. in any field in addition to at least 18 graduate level credit hours in Education or a closely related discipline, or If the Master’s Degree is in an unrelated field, the candidate must have a minimum of 5 years of successful experience in the field related to the course documented on the CV. Candidates need to supply some evidence (e.g., subject matter expertise or leadership expertise) of experience. Graduate Lecturer Minimum Qualifications MA Degree Programs • PhD, EdD, PsyD in Education, Counseling, Human Development, Family Relations, or closely related disciple field area. If the terminal degree is in an unrelated field, the candidate must demonstrate experience in the field related to the course documented on the CV Candidates need to supply some evidence of experience (e.g., subject matter expertise or leadership expertise). If the candidate does not have a terminal degree, the candidate must have a M.A. or M.S. AND a minimum of 5 years of successful experience in the field related to the course documented on the CV. Candidates need to supply some evidence (e.g., teaching experience, school principal, board certified counselor, etc.) of experience PhD, EdD, PsyD Degree Programs • PhD, EdD, PsyD in Education, Counseling, Human Development, Family Relations, or closely related disciple field area. If the doctorate degree is in an unrelated field, the candidate must demonstrate at least 18 graduate level credit hours in related discipline and/or 5 years’ experience in the field related to the course documented on the CV. Candidates need to supply some evidence (e.g., subject matter expertise or leadership expertise) of experience. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) • Ed.D. or Ph.D. in Education or a closely related field. • Experience teaching at the graduate level. • Experience teaching online or with blended/hybrid models (e.g. Blackboard or eCollege and LiveText. • Experience with culturally, linguistically, and economically diverse communities, knowledge of urban educational community issues, and an interest in collaborating with a variety of constituencies in an urban environment. • Proficient with basic technology (e.g. MS Office) CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $5,125 for a fully-enrolled 3-credit course. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. As of January 1, 2021, Lecturer positions will accrue sick leave at a rate of 0.034 hours per hour worked with a maximum accrual of 48 hours per fiscal year (July 1 – June 30). This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. As this is a pool posting, multiple hires will be made, and the posting will reopen periodically throughout the year to support semester-based hiring. It is typically open during the following timeframes: mid-February through April, June through July, and September through October. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: • A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. • Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). • Five professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). Questions should be directed to SEHD HR, sehdhr@ucdenver.edu Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
