    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41654&lang=en": {
      "title": "Lecturer of 3D Graphics & Animation",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/23/2026 Posted Date: Nov 9, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CAM-Visual Arts Schedule: Part-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Lecturer Working Title: Lecturer of 3D Graphics & Animation FTE: . 225 per 3 credit course Salary Range : $5,371.19 per 3 credit course Position #00638738 – Requisition #41654 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado - Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $800 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * At the College of Arts & Media (CAM), we empower a diverse community of makers, scholars, and innovators to thrive in the rapidly evolving creative industries. Through a dynamic blend of critical inquiry, creative exploration, and real-world engagement, CAM prepares students for lives filled with opportunity, meaning, and purpose. Rooted in community and driven by a spirit of intellectual and artistic freedom, CAM embraces risk-takers, visionaries, and cultural disruptors by providing access to cutting-edge technology, global perspectives, and a nurturing environment where bold ideas flourish. CAM offers top-ranked degrees and programs in Film & Television, Media Forensics, Music & Entertainment, and Visual Arts. In Visual Arts at CU Denver, the city becomes our canvas, industry leaders become professors and a passion becomes a profession. We do our best work designing in a digital lab or putting paint to canvas in a studio. Our students have access to state-of-the art digital labs, sculpture studios, industrial equipment and darkrooms. They find opportunities at the Denver Art Museum, in galleries across the Front Range and at digital studios like Pixar. That’s how the College of Arts & Media unites the ability to create with the means to succeed. 3D Graphics & Animation at CU Denver, recognized as one of the top 50 national programs by Animation Career Review, trains students to be 3D digital content creators—in film, entertainment, medicine, and science. Students graduate with instruction in arts aesthetics, critical thinking, and communication skills while providing an intense level of expertise, instruction, and immersion in digital 3D animation and graphics. Lecturer appointments are at-will and teaching assignments are determined on a semester by semester basis. Teaching assignments may fluctuate and are contingent upon the schedule of courses offered each term. Lecturer – 3D Graphics and Animation What you will do: 100% Teaching Lecturers may teach one or two classes (3-6 credit hours) per semester. Lecturers in the 3D Graphics & Animation Program may be expected to teach in one or more of the following areas at an introductory, intermediate, or advanced level: 3D modeling/sculpture 3D character animation 2D character animation Look Development, texturing emphasis Look Development, lighting emphasis Story and project development Animated film production Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Bachelor’s degree in 3D Graphics & Animation or similar major/emphasis. One year of relevant and demonstrable professional experience in the animation, visual effects, or game production industry. Proficiency with commercial software applications commonly used in the animation, visual effects, or game production industry that are relevant to the areas in which you specialize. Examples of such software applications are: Autodesk Maya Adobe Creative Cloud Applications (Photoshop, Substance 3D Painter, etc.) SideFX Houdini Foundry Production Collective Epic Games Unreal Engine Toon Boom Harmony Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) One year of relevant and demonstrable experience teaching in higher or K-12 education. Master’s degree in 3D Graphics & Animation or similar major/emphasis. Three years of relevant and demonstrable professional experience in the animation, visual effects, or game production industry. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Primary experience as a creative and/or producer in a collaborative media production environment. Strong interpersonal and communication skills across multiple teaching modalities Demonstrated history of building and maintaining effective working relationships with colleagues, students, and community partners representing a wide range of perspectives Ability to work autonomously, taking initiative on tasks, and meeting deadlines Outwardly contribute to a respectful, positive, and professional workspace Interest in emerging trends and technologies in illustration education and practice Demonstrable creative and aesthetic sensibility as it relates to 3D graphics & animation. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $5,371.19 per 3 credit course. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. This position is not eligible for benefits. Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until finalists are identified, and interviews begin. Preference will be given to complete applications received by November 23rd, 2026. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: Letter of application specifically addressing the position requirements and outlining qualifications Current CV or résumé Questions should be directed to Digital Animation Program Director, Jeremy Brown jeremy.d.brown@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41651&lang=en": {
      "title": "Lecturer of Performance (Voice)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/10/2027 Posted Date: Nov 9, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CAM-Music & Entertainment Schedule: Part-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Lecturer Working Title: Lecturer of Performance (Voice) FTE: .33 per half hour student, .66 per hour student Salary Range: $520.20 per half hour student, $1040.40 per hour student Position #00726509– Requisition #41651 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado - Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $800 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * At the College of Arts & Media (CAM), we empower a diverse community of makers, scholars, and innovators to thrive in the rapidly evolving creative industries. Through a dynamic blend of critical inquiry, creative exploration, and real-world engagement, CAM prepares students for lives filled with opportunity, meaning, and purpose. Rooted in community and driven by a spirit of intellectual and artistic freedom, CAM embraces risk-takers, visionaries, and cultural disruptors by providing access to cutting-edge technology, global perspectives, and a nurturing environment where bold ideas flourish. CAM offers top-ranked degrees and programs in Film & Television, Media Forensics, Music & Entertainment, and Visual Arts. The Music & Entertainment Industry Studies (MEIS) Department features some of the best contemporary-focused music industry programs in the country. With about 500 students, MEIS has one of the biggest music business, recording arts, singer/songwriter, and contemporary performance programs in the Western U.S. Our downtown Denver location is perfect for enabling our students to be very involved with the thriving Denver music scene while they are in the program. As one of the only music departments in the country with a contemporary focus, Music & Entertainment Industry Studies (MEIS) at CU Denver provides students with the guidance, knowledge, and expertise needed to transform passion for music into a lifelong profession. Applications for this pooled position will be accepted on a rolling basis for up to three (3) months from the posting date (TAC, please add posting date here) Qualified applicants may be reviewed and approved for hire at any point during this period. Early application is encouraged, as the pool may close before the three month window if all openings are filled. Lecturer appointments are at-will and teaching assignments are determined on a semester by semester basis. Teaching assignments may fluctuate and are contingent upon the schedule of courses offered each term. Lecturer of Performance (Voice) What you will do: 100% Teaching (Maximum 6 credit hours) As a Lecturer of Performance (Voice), your primary focus will be on inspiring and training the next generation of contemporary drum kit players. This can include instruction in the following: Lower division half hour voice lessons Upper division full hour voice lessons Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. BA/BS/BM in Music or related field Or tested experience as exemplified by one or more of the following: Recording with a reputable label (corresponding review from a reputable media outlet) Extensive experience as a performer/composer/arranger Representation with a reputable artist management company which contains an active roster of high level artists Evidence of a national/international tour/festival Evidence of song published/performed by a major artist/publishing/media outlet (i.e. film or TV show) or company Two years of successful experience teaching at a major university or college in same or similar program High level voice performance experience Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Master’s Degree in Music or related field Experience teaching music at the college level Experience teaching and performing multiple genre styles or style at a high level Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Proven experience in establishing effective working relationships with a diverse population at all levels throughout the institution—and in the community. Ability to communicate in a positive and effective manner, across various modalities. Ability to balance multiple projects and tasks while maintaining attention to detail. Ability to create equitable and positive experiences for all. Outwardly contribute to a respectful, positive, and professional workspace. Demonstrate an exceptional understanding of access and engagement with a commitment to developing equitable practices. Ability to establish work priorities, follow through, and meet established deadlines. Strong judgment and critical thinking skills. Ability to authentically listen and communicate relevant information, anticipate problems, and offer suggestions for resolution. Demonstrated dedication to developing professional relationships with students and alumni. Demonstrate dedication to mentoring students to best realize their potential as artists in society. Commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusivity equitable practices, and inclusive pedagogy Demonstrated excellence and experience in organizational teamwork, communication, and interpersonal skills CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $520.20 per half hour student, $1040.40 per hour student The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. This position is not eligible for benefits. Application Deadline Applications for this pooled position will be accepted on a rolling basis for up to three (3) months from the posting date until February 9th, 2027. Qualified applicants may be reviewed and approved for hire at any point during this period. Early application is encouraged, as the pool may close before the three (3) month window if all openings are filled. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: Letter of application specifically addressing the position requirements and outlining qualifications Current CV or résumé Questions should be directed to Evan Shelton, evan.shelton@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41632&lang=en": {
      "title": "Principal Talent Acquisitions & Compensation (TAC) Partner [Term Limited]",
      "description": "Closing Date: 11/17/2026 Posted Date: Nov 6, 2026 Job category: University Staff Department: ADM AFSVC Human Res-Denver Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Official Title: Human Resources Talent Acquisitions Principal Professional [Term Limited] Working Title: Principal Talent Acquisitions & Compensation (TAC) Partner [Term Limited] FTE: Full-time 1.0 (100%) FTE [Term Limited] | Hybrid Salary Range: $80,000-$85,000 Position #00854328 – Requisition #41632 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit cudenver.edu . *This position is Limited-Term through June 30, 2028, and subject to available funding.* Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Talent Acquisition and Compensation (TAC) Partner supports the Denver campus and serves as a key resource in talent acquisition, job evaluation, classification, and compensation for University Staff, Classified Staff, and Faculty positions. The TAC Partner provides consultation and guidance to HR Business Partners and hiring managers and applies knowledge of university systems, policies, processes, and employment practices to address a variety of workforce needs. This is a limited-term, project-based assignment designed to support strategic institutional initiatives and evolving workforce needs. The position will play a key role in supporting organizational and workforce alignment efforts, including position evaluations, job description development and redesign, classification reviews, compensation analysis, and implementation of workforce-related changes. The TAC Partner will collaborate with HR Business Partners, campus leadership, and other stakeholders to ensure positions, responsibilities, and compensation practices align with institutional priorities and organizational needs. This position reports to the Director for Talent Acquisition and Compensation. Talent Acquisition and Compensation Partner What you will do: Strategic Projects & Organizational Alignment Support high-impact institutional initiatives focused on organizational transformation, workforce alignment, and implementation of strategic priorities. Lead or coordinate comprehensive position review and job redesign efforts to support evolving organizational structures, operational needs, and institutional objectives. Collaborate with HR Business Partners, campus leadership, and cross-functional stakeholders to assess organizational impacts, identify opportunities for consistency and alignment, and develop recommendations to support workforce transitions. Develop and maintain project plans, documentation, recommendations, and other deliverables to support informed decision-making, stakeholder engagement, and successful implementation. Coordinate project activities, timelines, and milestones; identify and address implementation challenges and support effective execution of organizational changes. Talent Acquisition & Compensation Partner with school/college/unit (S/C/U) HR Business Partners and hiring managers to provide consultation services, such as developing and implementing recruitment and selection strategies, including proactive sourcing and full-cycle recruitment support. Evaluate positions and advise S/C/U HR Business Partners and hiring managers on position management, job evaluation, classification, and compensation, including determining appropriate position type, career family, professional level, classification, and title. Also includes reviewing Classified Staff position reviews, such as position reallocation requests Conduct job evaluations and market pricing using internal and external compensation data to support competitive, equitable, and consistent compensation decisions. Contribute to the development, administration, and ongoing maintenance of the university’s compensation program, including market pricing, salary structures, compensation practices, and analysis of internal and external market trends. Partner with campus stakeholders on faculty recruitment and compensation, including recruitment and hiring strategies, market pricing and compensation analysis, and interpretation and application of applicable university and Regent policies and procedures. Provide consultation on recruitment, selection, job evaluation, classification, and compensation for University Staff and Classified Staff. Ensure compliance with applicable requirements, including the Colorado Equal Pay for Equal Work Act, FLSA, State of Colorado personnel rules and procedures, Regent requirements, and university policies. Develop and deliver training, tools, communications, and guidance for HR Business Partners and hiring managers; support change management and adoption of new or revised HR programs, processes, practices, systems, and tools. Serves as a recognized subject-matter expert independently resolving highly complex, sensitive, or non-routine talent acquisition, job evaluation, classification, and compensation matters. Leads or supports complex, high-impact institutional projects involving organizational alignment, position evaluation, job redesign, classification, and compensation, providing advanced consultation and recommendations to campus stakeholders. Exercises independent judgment in evaluating complex position structures, identifying classification and compensation considerations, and developing solutions that support institutional priorities and consistent HR practices. Serves as a technical resource to other HR professionals and represents the Talent Acquisition and Compensation team on cross-functional initiatives requiring advanced subject-matter expertise. In collaboration with the Director, lead change management efforts for significant talent acquisition and compensation initiatives, including stakeholder engagement, communication, training, implementation, and adoption of new or revised programs, processes, systems, and practices. This description is not intended to be an exhaustive list of duties. Other duties may be assigned that are appropriate to the position and professional level. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. A bachelor's degree in Human Resource Management, Business Administration, Public Administration, Public Policy, Higher Education Administration, Social Sciences, Behavioral Sciences, Human Relations, or a directly related field from an accredited institution. Three (3) years of professional experience in talent acquisition/full-cycle recruitment, job evaluation, classification, compensation, or directly related human resources work. Demonstrated experience performing advanced or complex professional work requiring significant independent judgment and subject-matter expertise. Substitution: Related technical or paraprofessional experience may be substituted for the bachelor's degree on a year-for-year basis. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) A master's degree or terminal degree in Human Resources, Business Administration, Public Administration, Public Policy, Higher Education Administration, Social Sciences, Behavioral Sciences, Human Relations, or a directly related field. Four (4) or more years of professional experience in talent acquisition/full-cycle recruitment, job evaluation, classification, compensation, or directly related human resources work. Talent acquisition, compensation, or job evaluation experience in higher education. Experience utilizing PageUp for position management, recruitment, applicant tracking, and hiring processes. Experience utilizing PeopleSoft or a comparable HCM system for employee transactions and/or HR data management. Advanced proficiency in Microsoft Excel, including experience using formulas, lookup functions, pivot tables, and other data analysis tools to analyze and interpret compensation, workforce, or other complex data. Experience applying State of Colorado personnel rules and procedures. State of Colorado certification in selection and/or job evaluation. Professional HR certification such as PHR, SPHR, SHRM-CP, SHRM-SCP, IPMA-CP, or IPMA-SCP. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with employees at all levels throughout the institution. Knowledge of the professional standards, concepts, and practices of recruitment and selection, employee relations, or organizational development and human resource policy. Demonstrated commitment and leadership ability to advance diversity and inclusion. Ability to analyze, interpret, and evaluate a broad range of laws, rules, and regulations to exercise good judgment in applying them to human resource challenges. Ability to deal with ambiguous situations and/or information and translate them into results. Thorough knowledge of State Classified systems including rules, technical guidance, and practices related to recruiting. Knowledge of the professional standards, concepts, and practices of recruitment and selection, employee relations, or organizational development and human resource policy. Ability to communicate effectively, both in writing and orally, including public speaking. Ability to adapt to change in organizations and provide clear communication with stakeholders through various stages of change. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with employees at all levels throughout the institution. Demonstrated commitment and leadership ability to advance diversity and inclusion Expert knowledge and experience of systems and processes. Thorough knowledge of current management and leadership methods and best practices Expert knowledge and experience of systems and processes. Ability to deal with ambiguous situations and/or information and translate them into results. Thorough knowledge of State Classified systems including rules, technical guidance, and practices related to recruiting. Conditions of Employment This position is Limited-Term through June 30, 2028, and subject to available funding. This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. This position will be required to attend local career fairs as a CU Denver representative. Incumbent will be required to obtain the State of Colorado Statewide Personnel Certification Program (SPCP) certification in Job Evaluation and Personnel Services Contracts within 12 months of hire. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed in a hybrid or remote environment, requiring the ability to work effectively in a remote setting. This includes maintaining a dedicated workspace free of excessive noise or distractions, reliable internet connectivity, and the ability to participate in virtual meetings for extended periods. Employees must be able to independently manage time, remain stationary for prolonged computer-based work, and transport necessary equipment (such as a laptop and peripherals) between their remote work location and the office as needed. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodations to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $75,000–$85,000.0 The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by November 16, 2026. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A cover letter which specifically addresses the job requirements and outlines qualifications A current CV/resume List of three references (we will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to Tawnya Boulier, tawnya.boulier@ucdenver.edu. Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41624&lang=en": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Digital Animation",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/06/2026 Posted Date: Nov 6, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CAM-Visual Arts Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Assistant Teaching Track Working Title: Assistant Teaching Professor of Digital Animation FTE: Full-time Salary Range: $78,000 - $88,000 Position #00668617 – Requisition #41624 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * At the College of Arts & Media (CAM), we empower a diverse community of makers, scholars, and innovators to thrive in the rapidly evolving creative industries. Through a dynamic blend of critical inquiry, creative exploration, and real-world engagement, CAM prepares students for lives filled with opportunity, meaning, and purpose. Rooted in community and driven by a spirit of intellectual and artistic freedom, CAM embraces risk-takers, visionaries, and cultural disruptors by providing access to cutting-edge technology, global perspectives, and a nurturing environment where bold ideas flourish. CAM offers top-ranked degrees and programs in Film & Television, Media Forensics, Music & Entertainment, and Visual Arts. The Visual Arts Department has an opening for a full-time Assistant Teaching Professor of Digital Animation in the Teaching Professor track (unclassified, non-tenure-track). The appointment will have a start date of August 2027 and will require a 3-3 teaching load. The incumbent will teach undergraduate courses across the Digital Animation curriculum in their area of expertise and will engage in research, creative work, and service. The continuance of this appointment will be based on merit/performance, budget, and enrollment numbers. The program also welcomes candidates whose expertise extends beyond what we currently teach. Areas of interest include virtual production, real-time lighting and rendering, layout and previsualization, motion capture, visual effects, and technical art. The successful candidate will have room to develop new courses in their area of strength. Digital Animation at CU Denver is embedded within the BFA in Fine Arts degree program with an emphasis in 3D Graphics and Animation, housed in the Digital Animation Center (DAC). The program culminates with a three-semester capstone in which student teams produce an animated short through an academic simulation of a studio pipeline. Student films have screened at more than 300 national and international festivals in 22 countries and have won more than 75 Best Animated Short Awards. CU Denver's animation program in the 2026 Animation Career Review rankings: #1 in Colorado Top 5 in the Southwest (#5) Top 40 nationally among public schools and colleges (#15) Top 50 nationally (#40) Assistant Teaching Professor of Digital Animation What you will do: Teaching 80% Teach three classes per semester (9 credit hours), for a total of six per academic year (18 credit hours). Teach required and elective courses in the Digital Animation curriculum, which may include: Digital Cinematography (FINE 2822) Introduction to Unreal Engine (FINE 3855) Creating Visual Story: Narrative Techniques and Visual Design (FINE 3841) DAC: Preproduction for Story (FINE 3845) International Perspectives through Animation (FINE 1002) or other course(s) that align with the candidate’s area of expertise Develop new or revised courses in the candidate's area of expertise as the curriculum and industry practice change. Faculty are responsible for the preparation of course syllabi, lectures, presentations, and student assignments; evaluation of student work; tracking and progress of student attendance and engagement; management of courses in the online platform Canvas; etc. Maintain office hours. Meet with Digital Animation colleagues about refinement of the curriculum to address current industry practice. Attend Visual Arts Faculty and CAM meetings. Research/Creative Practice 10% Maintain an active scholarly and/or creative practice in animation, film, games, or related field. Service 10% Engage in and contribute to the function and operations of the department, as defined by the department chair and in collaboration with the senior associate dean. Work with the broader community in representing the university, college, department, and students. All other duties and projects as requested. This description is a summary only and describes the general level of work being performed; it is not intended to be all-inclusive. The duties of this position may change from time to time and/or based on business need. We reserve the right to add or delete duties and tasks at the discretion of the supervisor and/or hiring authority. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. MFA or other terminal degree in animation, film, digital arts, visual effects, game art, or a related field and at least 3 years of significant professional experience in the animation, film, game, or related industry. OR Baccalaureate degree and at least five years of significant professional experience in the animation, film, game, or related industry. At least one year of successful teaching at the college level, which can include animation, modeling, look development, post-production, pre-production, narrative development, interactive/real-time/game production. Experience with industry-standard digital content creation applications including but not limited to: Maya and Adobe Creative Cloud Applications such as Photoshop and Premiere. An online portfolio or demo reel demonstrating professional quality work in applicant’s area of expertise with a breakdown of the applicant's role on each piece. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) 4 or more years of college-level teaching experience 6 or more years of professional experience in animation, visual effects, games, virtual production, or a related field Professional credits on released film, television, game, or commercial productions, or other demonstrable record of accomplishment in creative research and practice such as festival screenings or exhibitions. Experience working in or teaching a collaborative studio production pipeline. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of the 3D animation production pipeline from story and previsualization through layout, lighting, rendering, and post-production. Depth in at least one area of the pipeline, such as cinematography and layout, lighting and rendering, rigging, technical art, visual effects, or real-time production. Working knowledge of industry-standard 3D software and real-time engines (e.g., Unreal, Unity), or willingness to learn and teach them. Ability to teach and work with a diverse student body. Ability to communicate in a positive and effective manner, across various modalities. Proven experience in establishing and maintaining effective working relationships with a diverse population at all levels throughout the institution and into the community. Willingness to explore and teach new tools and trends in animation and real-time production. Ability to create equitable and positive experiences for all. Ability to mentor student teams working toward shared production deadlines. Broad knowledge of the history of animation, film, and contemporary visual storytelling. Ability to outwardly contribute to a respectful, positive, and professional workspace. Ability to work independently. Conditions of Employment This position requires an on-site presence on campus for teaching, student mentoring, and departmental engagement Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required for departmental events, screenings, or industry guest events Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position will be performed on site and will require the ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $78,000-$88,000. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training.The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline • Applications will be accepted until finalists are identified, and interviews begin. Preference will be given to complete applications received by December 6th, 2026. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A letter of interest specifically addressing the job requirements and outlining qualifications A current curriculum vitae / resume An online portfolio or demo reel demonstrating professional quality work in applicant’s area of expertise with a breakdown of the applicant's role on each piece A statement of teaching philosophy, with examples of student work if available. A list of five professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references) Questions should be directed to Erin Hauger, Erin.Hauger@UCDenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41635&lang=en": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor of Film Studies",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/07/2026 Posted Date: Nov 6, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CAM-Music & Entertainment Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Assistant Teaching Track Working Title: Assistant Teaching Professor of Film Studies FTE: Full-time Salary Range: $62,000 - $67,000 Position #00672409 – Requisition #41635 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu. Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * At the College of Arts & Media (CAM), we empower a diverse community of makers, scholars, and innovators to thrive in the rapidly evolving creative industries. Through a dynamic blend of critical inquiry, creative exploration, and real-world engagement, CAM prepares students for lives filled with opportunity, meaning, and purpose. Rooted in community and driven by a spirit of intellectual and artistic freedom, CAM embraces risk-takers, visionaries, and cultural disruptors by providing access to cutting-edge technology, global perspectives, and a nurturing environment where bold ideas flourish. CAM offers top-ranked degrees and programs in Film & Television, Media Forensics, Music & Entertainment, and Visual Arts. The Department of Film & Television within the College of Arts & Media invites dynamic educators, film scholars, and creative professionals to apply for an open-rank, full-time Teaching Track position specializing in Film History, Film Theory, and Film Criticism. We seek a colleague with strong scholarly credentials alongside deep industry connections, capable of bridging critical academic discourse with contemporary industry perspectives. Why Join CU Denver CAM? Be part of a forward-looking, supportive faculty committed to excellence and innovation. Enjoy a culture that values your unique artistic and academic contributions. Teach within a nationally recognized program. Shape a vibrant program positioned at the crossroads of professional opportunity and creative experimentation, in a city alive with storytelling and artistic energy. The Film & Television program offers a Bachelor of Fine Arts (BFA) in Film and Television. This BFA emphasis provides intensive, practical training that requires successful completion of numerous film/TV projects, ranging from fiction to documentary. The emphasis is designed for students to progress through the semesters as a cohort. Undergraduates will work on their own projects and assist with numerous group projects. Students graduate with both a theoretical and historical perspective, as well as hands-on experience in film and television production. This program prepares students to begin their careers at entry-level positions in the film or television industry, start their personal creative journey, or continue their studies in a graduate program. We believe in the power of transformative education, hands-on learning, and creative exploration. Our BFA program cultivates storytellers with a strong technical foundation and artistic vision, preparing graduates for immediate impact in the film, television, and media industries. Accolades: For five consecutive years, CU Denver’s Film & Television program has been named to MovieMaker Magazine’s Best Film Schools list. We are recognized for our hands-on, cohort-based curriculum, affordability, and strong industry connections. Career Pathways & Networking: Graduates are successful across multiple areas, including opportunities in Hollywood, independent film, commercial film, and post-production studios. Hands-on from Day One: The curriculum is sequence-based, allowing students to build on skill sets developed throughout their college career. Students Building a High-Volume Portfolio: The curriculum is known for its rigor, as students often complete 20+ films/exercises for their portfolios by graduation. This indicates a highly engaged and active student body. Access to Professional Gear: The program’s “film cage” provides students with access to industry-standard, professional-grade cameras, sound, lighting, and grip equipment. Collaborative Cohort Culture: The program is built on a cohort-style curriculum in which students build strong bonds by working on each other’s film sets, emphasizing collegiality and team-based teaching and learning. Integrated Acting Emphasis: The new Acting for the Screen BFA emphasis is fully integrated with the filmmaking students. This intentional structure means acting in student films is a part of their training, fostering essential on-set collaboration between filmmakers and actors. We seek individuals with a passion for teaching and the ability to mentor and inspire students in a dynamic learning environment. If you’re ready to inspire the next generation of media makers and enrich an inclusive academic community, we want to hear from you! Assistant Teaching Professor of Film Studies What you will do: 80% Teaching, 10% Scholarly/Research, 10% Service As an Assistant Teaching Professor, your primary focus will be delivering engaging, rigorous instruction in Film History, Film Theory, and Film Criticism while leveraging industry connections to enhance the academic and professional development of our BFA students. Teaching 80% Teaching Load: Teach three classes per semester (9 credit hours), for a total of six per academic year (18 credit hours). Core Instruction: Teach introductory and intermediate undergraduate courses in Film History, Film Theory, and Film Criticism. Advanced Instruction: Develop and deliver specialized upper-division seminars in critical studies, genre analysis, auteur studies, contemporary global cinema, or industry practices. Program Development: Guide the growth and integration of critical studies coursework within the BFA production curriculum in concert with other departmental priorities. Industry Engagement & Outreach: Expand opportunities for students in the department to connect with industry professionals through initiatives such as a curated extracurricular speaker series, community outreach, and other engagement events that foster meaningful connections with the field. Scholarly/Research 10% • Maintain an active scholarly, research, and/or creative practice in film studies, critical writing, or media history. Service 10% Engage in departmental, college, and university service activities, including student advising, curriculum review, committee work, and other forms of service to the college, community, and profession. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Education: a bachelor’s degree in film studies, cinema/media Studies, or a closely related field. Published Work: a record of professional distinction and recognized expertise in the field, demonstrated through a substantial body of published work such as books, film criticism, essays, cultural journalism, public-facing scholarship, or other significant writing on film, media, or related subjects (e.g., three (3) significant published pieces or one (1) book). Teaching: One (1) year of successful university-level teaching experience. Industry Experience: Two (2) years in mid-level, production, or editorial roles. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Education: Ph.D. or terminal degree in film studies, cinema/media studies, or a related discipline. Teaching: Three (3) years of successful university-level teaching experience, with a demonstrated record of teaching excellence, pedagogical development, and curriculum contribution. Experience integrating critical theory and historical perspectives into a production-focused BFA curriculum. Experience providing academic guidance, mentoring, tutoring, or instructional support to students in an educational setting. Industry Experience: substantial professional accomplishment and recognized expertise in the field, demonstrated through extensive industry experience; experience in senior, lead, or leadership roles with demonstrated field impact. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge: Deep understanding of foundational and contemporary film theory, world film history, global movements, and critical frameworks; strong understanding of current media industry trends and distribution landscapes. Skills: Excellent oral and written communication skills; effective pedagogical techniques for engaging BFA production students in critical analysis; strong networking and organizational abilities to build external industry partnerships. Ability: Ability to articulate complex theoretical and historical concepts clearly; ability to mentor and advise undergraduate students on academic and career trajectories; ability to work collaboratively within a cohort-based program structure. Conditions of Employment This position requires an onsite presence on campus for teaching, student mentoring, and departmental engagement. Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required for departmental events, screenings, or industry guest events. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position will be performed on site and will require the ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at $62,000 - $67,000. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation.Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until finalists are identified, and interviews begin. Preference will be given to complete applications received by December 6th, 2026. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). Five professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). Questions should be directed to David Liban, David.Liban@UCDenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41647&lang=en": {
      "title": "Lecturer [Part-time]",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/09/2027 Posted Date: Nov 10, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CLAS-Sociology Schedule: Part-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Lecturer [Part-time] Working Title: Lecturer FTE: Part time Salary Range: Requested Salary Range: standard CLAS lecturer pay rates. The proposed hiring rate for this position is $1,704 per credit hour for undergraduate courses and $1,849 per credit hour for graduate courses, per semester. Position #00713525– Requisition #41647 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * This posting will establish and maintain a pool of part-time lecturers with experience teaching sociology courses within their area of expertise and courses for the new Bachelor of Social Work for the Department of Sociology in the College of Liberal Arts and Sciences at the University of Colorado Denver. Lecturers may be asked to teach on campus or fully online. This position will be supervised by the Sociology department chair. By submitting your application, you will become part of an applicant pool. Candidates hired from this pool will fill temporary, non-tenure track positions and will teach no more than two courses per semester. Appointments will be part-time (less than 50%) and will be made semester-by-semester, depending on the needs of the department and subject to budgetary approval. Therefore, this posting does not necessarily reflect a current open position. We will contact you if an opening suits your qualifications. Lecturer What you will do: Key Responsibilities: Support student learning through effective, engaging instruction. Prepare and deliver instructional materials, lectures, discussions, and activities appropriate to course content and instructional modality. Develop and maintain course syllabi, assignments, assessments, and grading practices consistent with university and departmental policies. Evaluate student work and provide timely, constructive feedback on student learning and performance. Maintain regular communication with students, including scheduled office hours or other appropriate opportunities for academic support. Use the university's learning management system, Canvas, to organize course materials, communicate with students, and manage assignments and assessments. Apply effective teaching approaches that encourage critical thinking, sociological analysis, and engagement with evidence-based explanations of social phenomena. Participate in course coordination and communicate with departmental leadership regarding instructional needs, as appropriate. Comply with university policies governing academic instruction, student evaluation, and professional conduct. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Master's degree in Sociology or Social Work or a closely related field from an accredited institution. Graduate-level preparation or demonstrated subject-matter expertise relevant to the course or courses assigned. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) 3 years prior teaching experience at the college or university level. Experience with online or hybrid instruction and learning management systems such as Canvas. Experience using engaged, experiential, or active-learning instructional methods. Demonstrated ability to support student learning among students with varied educational backgrounds and experiences. Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Knowledge of sociological and social work concepts, theories, research methods, and contemporary issues relevant to the assigned courses. Ability to communicate complex sociological and social work concepts clearly and effectively to college students. Strong written and oral communication skills. Ability to create an academically rigorous and supportive learning environment. Ability to evaluate student learning and provide meaningful, timely feedback. Ability to organize course materials, meet instructional deadlines, and maintain accurate academic records. Ability to use instructional technologies effectively, including learning management systems and online communication tools. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with students, faculty, and staff. Conditions of Employment Appointments are temporary, part-time, and non-tenure-track, subject to instructional needs and available funding. Teaching assignments may require an onsite presence at the CU Denver campus or may be offered through hybrid or remote modalities, depending on course requirements. Occasional evening teaching assignments may be required, depending on departmental scheduling needs. Continued appointments are not guaranteed and are dependent on course availability, satisfactory performance, and departmental needs. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements Ability to perform instructional and administrative work using a computer and associated technology. Ability to sit or stand for periods associated with classroom instruction, meetings, and computer-based work. Ability to communicate effectively with students and colleagues in instructional settings, with reasonable accommodations as appropriate. For in-person courses, ability to perform the essential functions of classroom instruction on campus, with or without reasonable accommodation. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established based upon standard CLAS lecturer pay rates. The proposed hiring rate for this position is $1,704 per credit hour for undergraduate courses and $1,849 per credit hour for graduate courses, per semester. The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation or benefits. Application Deadline Applications for this pooled position will be accepted on a rolling basis for up to three (3) months from the posting date 11/10/26. Qualified applicants may be reviewed and approved for hire at any point during this period. Early application is encouraged, as the pool may close before the three‑month window if all openings are filled. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). Three professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). Questions should be directed to Carmen.Jura@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41699&lang=en": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor or Senior Instructor(Open Rank)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 02/28/2027 Posted Date: Nov 11, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CEDC-Computer SC & Engineering Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Assistant Teaching Professor (Open Rank) Working Title: Assistant Teaching Professor or Senior Instructor(Open Rank) FTE: Full-time Salary Range: Senior Instructor: $60,000-$80,000 | Assistant Teaching Professor: $70,000-$90,000 Position #00770509 and 00781662 – Requisition #41699 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * About the College The College of Engineering, Design and Computing takes pride in having a faculty of excellent scholars and engineering practitioners. The college values innovation, creativity and an interdisciplinary approach to engineering and research. We focus on providing our students with a comprehensive engineering education at the undergraduate, graduate and professional level. Faculty conduct research that spans our five disciplines of civil engineering and construction, electrical and mechanical engineering, biomedical engineering, and computer science and engineering. The college collaborates with industry from around the state; our laboratories and research opportunities give students the hands-on experience they need to excel in the professional world. About the Department The Computer Science and Engineering Department (CSE) currently has sixteen full-time faculty members. The department offers a ABET accredited Bachelor of Science degree in Computer Science, a Bachelor of Arts degree in Computer Science, a Bachelor of Science degree in Cybersecurity, a Master of Science degree in Computer Science, and two multidisciplinary PhD degrees in Computer Science and Information Systems and Engineering and Applied Science, and several certificates. The CSE department provides quality computer science education and research opportunities to our students and quality professional service to the professional community and the general public in the Denver metropolitan area. The current strengths of the faculty are in the areas of algorithms, artificial intelligence, computer networks, parallel and distributed systems, mobile systems, machine learning, Cybersecurity and secure computing, data science and AI, satellite communication and theory. Our faculty are engaged in multidisciplinary research and encourage collaborative research among disciplines. In addition to general computing labs, the department houses several specialized research labs in Next Generation Networking & Security, Cyber-Physical Systems (CPS), Active Cyber Infrastructure and Defense (ACID), Machine Learning (ML), networks (DECENT), Big Data Management & Mining Laboratory (BDLab), Neuroscience & AI, and parallel and distributed systems (PDS). Assistant Teaching Professor or Senior Instructor (Open Rank) What you will do: The Department of Computer Science and Engineering in the College of Engineering, Design and Computing at the University of Colorado Denver invites applications for a non-tenure track faculty position at the level Assistant Teaching Professor or Senior Instructor. The major responsibility of the individual in this position will be developing and teaching courses in the undergraduate computer science program in Computer Science and Engineering. The individual will conduct scholarly research in their area of expertise to be current with the rapid changes in the field and contribute to the academic success of students through advising and student project supervision. Teaching assignments may include online, evening and Friday courses. In addition to teaching existing courses, they will contribute to the ongoing development and modernization of the Computer Science curricula. This includes updating existing courses and developing new courses that incorporate emerging technologies, methods, and areas of practice in computer science. Areas of teaching and curriculum development may span the breadth of computer science, including computer architecture and organization, assembly language and systems programming, logic and digital systems, operating systems, computer networks, algorithms and theoretical foundations, software engineering, artificial intelligence and machine learning, data science, cloud computing, cybersecurity, and other emerging areas. These efforts will help ensure that the program provides a strong foundation in core computer science while remaining current and responsive to evolving technological and workforce needs across business, industry, healthcare, government, and other organizations. This is a 9-month academic year (AY) teaching-track faculty appointment with 80% teaching (eight courses per AY) and 20% service. Research is not an assigned component of the workload. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. This is an open-rank position with the following required qualifications: Assistant Teaching Professor: PhD in Computer Science, Computer Science and Engineering or closely related field. Demonstrated expertise and track record of teaching accomplishments commensurate with the candidate’s rank in computer science, as evidenced by their record. Senior Instructor: Master’s degree in Computer Science, Computer Science and Engineering or closely related field with study and experience in computer science. Demonstrated expertise and track record of teaching accomplishments commensurate with the candidate’s rank in computer science, as evidenced by their record. Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) Strong interest in curriculum development and teaching undergraduate courses Passion for mentoring and supervising undergraduate student projects Experience advising and mentoring students Teaching experience at the undergraduate level Experience in program and course development Scholarly research skills and experience in computer science. Conditions of Employment Occasional work during the evening and/or weekends may be required. This position requires an onsite presence on campus 5 days a week. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed on site, and will require the ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. T he University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at: Senior Instructor: $60,000-$80,000 Assistant Teaching Professor: $70,000-$90,000 The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line. Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by February 1, 2027. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). One (1) PDF containing the following documents combined (may not exceed 2.5 megabytes): A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. Statement of teaching philosophy, experience, and interests (3-page limit). List of three professional references including name, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting references). Optional but encouraged: Statement of research and development, experience, and interests (3-page limit). Questions should be directed to Kelly Konrad, kelly.p.konrad@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    },
    "https://cu.taleo.net/careersection/2/jobdetail.ftl?job=41701&lang=en": {
      "title": "(Open Rank) Instructor/GAM Lab Director",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/31/2026 Posted Date: Nov 11, 2026 Job category: Faculty Department: CLAS-GEOGRAPHY/ENVIRNMENTL SCI Schedule: Full-time Position Details University of Colorado | Denver Faculty Level/Title: Instructor or Senior Instructor (Open Rank) Working Title: (Open Rank) Instructor /GAM Lab Director FTE: Full Time 1.0 FTE | Hybird Salary Range: Instructor/GAM Lab Director ($60,000-$64,000), Senior Instructor/GAM Lab Director ($65,000-$69,000) Position #00768665 – Requisition #41701 Join the University of Colorado Denver About the University of Colorado Denver Millions of moments start at CU Denver, a place where innovation, research, and learning meet in the heart of a global city. We’re the state’s premier public urban research university with more than 100 in-demand, top-ranked bachelor’s, master’s, and doctoral degree programs. We partner with diverse learners—at any stage of their life and career—for transformative educational experiences. Across seven schools and colleges, our leading faculty inspires and works alongside students to solve complex challenges and produce impactful creative work. As part of the state’s largest university system, CU Denver is a major contributor to the Colorado economy, with 2,000 employees and an annual economic impact of $665 million. To learn more about how CU Denver helps learners meet their moment, visit ucdenver.edu . Job Description * Applications are accepted electronically ONLY at www.cu.edu/cu-careers * The Department of Geography and Environmental Sciences at the University of Colorado Denver invites applications for an open rank non-tenure track Instructor or Senior Instructor to manage and maintain the Geospatial Analysis and Mapping Laboratory (GAMLab). The position is located in Denver, Colorado and is not remote. This is a full-time (9 months) academic year faculty appointment. Summer work may be available as an additional appointment. Time allocation for this position is 30% formal teaching (equivalent to 3 classes per year, either two in the fall and one in the spring semester, or vice versa), 20% informal teaching (equivalent to student advising in the GAM open lab and GIS certificate program), 40% management of the GAM lab operations (including sourcing client-based research projects, overseeing student researchers, managing requests for geospatial support and services), and 10% service in the department. The successful candidate will be prepared to teach required courses within the undergraduate and graduate geospatial curriculum as well as within an existing emphasis area. The position requires a flexible teaching schedule to include day and evening classes as well as the ability to teach in a variety of formats (e.g., face-to-face, hybrid, and online). This position reports to the Chair of Geography & Environmental Sciences. The GAMLab provides resources and services to visualize, explore, analyze, and propose solutions to human-environmental challenges using Geographic Information Science and Technology. The GAMLab is housed in GES and it supports the geospatial research and teaching activities of CU Denver's faculty and students, in addition to facilitating collaborations within/between GES and other members of academic, government, private, and community sectors. The successful candidate will be dynamic, innovative, and possess a passion for formal (e.g., classroom) and informal (e.g., student mentoring and advising) teaching, as well as a managerial and entrepreneurial spirit to advance the mission of the GAMlab. In addition, the candidate will build new and maintain current relationships with faculty in GES and affiliated departments/colleges, as well as develop awareness of the lab and its services with the Denver and the Colorado Front Range communities through networking activities and events. Instructor or Senior Instructor/GAMLab Director What you will do: Teach three undergraduate or graduate geospatial classes each academic year. Provide geospatial science support of faculty and student research projects. Develop training opportunities (e.g. workshops). Generate contracts for cartographic and geospatial work performed in the lab. Build productive relationships with faculty in GES and affiliated departments/colleges as well as create awareness of the lab and its services to the Denver community through networking activities and events. Manage the client-based operations and budget of the Geospatial Mapping and Analysis Laboratory (GAMLab). Supervise open lab hours for students engaged in geospatial work. Oversee and coordinate students working on contract projects and in open lab hours. Serve as academic advisor for geospatial coursework and the Geographic Information Systems (GIS) certificate. Dedicate 10% of work time towards university service, such as serving on curriculum committees or similar. Qualifications you already possess (Minimum Qualifications) Applicants must meet minimum qualifications at the time of hire. Instructor/GAM Lab Director Master’s degree in Geography, Geospatial Science, Environmental Science, Urban Planning, or related field from an accredited institution. Experience using geospatial applications such as ArcGIS Pro, FOSS4G, OpenAI At least two years’ experience teaching geospatial courses in higher education. Senior Instructor/GAM Lab Director Master’s degree in Geography, Geospatial Science, Environmental Science, Urban Planning, or related field from an accredited institution. Experience using geospatial applications such as ArcGIS Pro, FOSS4G, OpenAI At least five years’ experience teaching geospatial courses in higher education Preferred Qualification to possess (Preferred Qualifications) A doctorate (PhD) in geography or a related field Experience with student advising and support of geospatial research projects Applied experience with cartographic design and geospatial analysis and at the Senior Instructor level, demonstrated professional background in a specific emphasis area of geospatial science and technology Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Expert knowledge of recent developments in Geographic Information Systems (GIS) and Remote Sensing technologies Knowledge and experience with recent developments in geospatial science and technology, such as machine learning and GeoAI, drone and LIDAR operations Demonstrated ability to generate revenue-producing activities through funded grants and contracts Familiarity and connections with GIS professional community in Denver/Colorado. Successful candidates must have demonstrated success teaching geospatial classes in professional or higher education contexts. Ability to communicate effectively, both in writing and orally. Ability to establish and maintain effective working relationships with employees and supervisors at all levels throughout the institution. Excellent pedagogical skills and the interest as well as capacity to teach a range (e.g., undergraduate, graduate) of geospatial courses. Outstanding customer service and interpersonal skills. Demonstrate an exceptional understanding of access and engagement with a commitment to developing equitable practices Ability to provide coordination and oversight of student team activities. Conditions of Employment This position follows a hybrid work structure where employees can work remotely or from the office, as needed, based on demands of specific tasks or personal work preferences. Working from the office is encouraged when working on tasks that require a high degree of collaboration. Mental, Physical, and/or Environmental Requirements This position may be performed on site and will require the ability to sit for extended periods, stand and walk occasionally, reach with hands and arms, use hands to manipulate a keyboard and mouse, and have good near vision for computer work; often requiring minimal lifting, but may involve bending or reaching to retrieve items from shelves or drawers. CU is an Equal Opportunity Employer and complies with all applicable federal, state, and local laws governing nondiscrimination in employment. We are committed to creating a workplace where all individuals are treated with respect and dignity, and we encourage individuals from all backgrounds to apply, including protected veterans and individuals with disabilities. The University will provide reasonable accommodation to applicants with disabilities throughout the employment application process. To request an accommodation pursuant to the Americans with Disabilities Act, please contact the Human Resources ADA Coordinator at workplaceengagement@ucdenver.edu . Employment Sponsorship Please be advised that this position is not eligible now or in the future for visa sponsorship. Compensation and Benefits The salary range (or hiring range) for this position has been established at Instructor/GAM Lab Director ($60,000-$64,000), Senior Instructor/GAM Lab Director ($65,000-$69,000) The salary of the finalist(s) selected for this role will be set based on a variety of factors, including but not limited to, internal equity, experience, education, specialty, and training. The above salary range (or hiring range) represents the University’s good faith and reasonable estimate of the range of possible compensation at the time of posting. This position is not eligible for overtime compensation. Your total compensation goes beyond the number on your paycheck. The University of Colorado Denver provides generous leave, health plans and retirement contributions that add to your bottom line.Benefits statement to include in all benefits-eligible positions. See Eligibility Matrix. Benefits: https://www.cu.edu/employee-services/benefits Total Compensation Calculator: http://www.cu.edu/node/153125 Application Deadline Applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Preference will be given to complete applications received by December 15, 2026. Those who do not apply by this date may or may not be considered. Required Application Materials: To apply, please visit: http://www.cu.edu/cu-careers and attach: A letter of interest describing relevant job experiences as they relate to listed job qualifications and interest in the position. Curriculum vitae / Resume outlining experience(s). A teaching statement describing teaching philosophy and experience, and pedagogical approaches as they relate to different types of geospatial courses. Five professional references including name, address, phone number (mobile number if appropriate), and email address. (We will notify you prior to contacting both on and off-list references). Additional materials can be added here if applicable. Questions should be directed to Peter Anthamatten at peter.anthamatten@ucdenver.edu . Background Check Policy The University of Colorado Denver strives to maintain a safe and productive educational, clinical, research and employment environment. All prospective employees and current employees must, therefore, consent to and pass background checks prior to any final appointment/employment."
    }
  },
